## 1. INTRODUCTION
Food safety is one of the biggest concerns faced worldwide. While developing countries are at higher risk of exposure, developed countries also require rigorous food safety standards to prevent exposure to food-borne pathogens. With globalization, many food products are distributed across borders, which increases the ease of food-borne pathogen transmission.2 There have been many reported incidents of contamination in recent years, including dioxin contaminated eggs, milk powder contaminated with melamine, and food products that carried the salmonella bacteria. As recently as 2017, the European Union reported the presence of a pesticide, fipronil, in batches of eggs in Belgium, Germany, and other European countries. A similar outbreak also occurred in Asia at around the same time. These incidents have had a huge impact on both consumers as well as national economies, resulting in significant attention across the globe. Food contamination is an issue that affects everyone, and action needs to be taken to ensure food safety before the product reaches the consumer. Thus, it is essential that we develop good systems to detect, remove, and control the risks caused by the presence of hazardous substances.
## 1.1. Risks and Hazards in the Food Supply Chain
The food supply chain spans from farm to table and is a multistep process that encompasses production, processing, preservation, storage, packaging, transportation, and consumption. There are many contaminants that pose a significant risk if they are not detected prior to consumption. These include heavy metals,3 veterinary residues,4 pesticides,5 environmental organic contaminants,6 mycotoxins,7 and bacterial pathogens,8 among many others.9 These substances can be introduced to food products at various stages of the process such as the cultivation of plant and animal sources (via the water, soil or feed), processing, preservation, or packaging.10,11 Due to the high risk of exposure to contaminants, food safety has become a crucial part of the developed world. Another important aspect of food safety is food spoilage, which is a huge contributing factor for both food wastage and food poisoning.12 Studies have shown that approximately one-third of food produced (about 1.3 billion metric tons per year) is wasted across the food supply chain. The leading factors that contribute to food wastage differ slightly between the developing and developed world. In developed countries, food waste is largely due to inadequate quality standards in food preservation and packaging. On the other hand, in the developing world, poor quality food products, food spoilage, and contamination are the leading factors.13 Logistics and supply chain management have played a crucial role in the food industry by providing traceability, which allows for improved quality control resulting from increased accountability.14,15 The most prominent improvement in recent years has been the advancements in technology and materials in this industry.
In addition, it is particularly important to note that water plays an important role in food safety. Water is not only an important substance to nourish human life but also an extremely valuable natural resource for food production. Hence, some hazardous substances in drinking water can directly threaten human health, and they may enter into the food chain through water in agricultural production and food processing to expose hazards to humans. In addition, water as an active molecule can result in food spoilage and deterioration to affect food safety. Therefore, providing technology to safe water is one of the most effective approaches in promoting human health and well-being.
## 1.2. Technologies and Associated Materials for Food Safety
There are many different subfields of food safety. Of these, legislation regarding safe levels of harmful chemicals present in foods, commonly referred as to maximum residue limits (MRL), provides us with information on the level of microbial related spoilage, presence of allergens, and detection of environmental contaminants as well as banned compounds, in addition to accessing the levels of naturally occurring toxins.16 There have been significant efforts toward the development and advancement of technologies used in the detection, removal, and control of risks to ensure food safety.17 Nanomaterials and nanotechnology are two areas that have seen significant development in this field.
Many techniques that employ nanotechnology to monitor food safety have been developed, ensuring that food distributed to the consumers is untainted. Of the materials studied, a few of the standouts include graphene oxide (GO), carbon nanotubes (CNT), and molecularly imprinted polymers (MIP). These materials have been used as adsorbents, enabling the extraction of unwanted compounds from the food sourc e.18 These materials have also been used as components in devices tailored for the detection of chemical and biological contaminants, allowing food products with significant levels of toxic compounds to be removed from the supply chain, minimizing the distribution of contaminated products to the consumer.19
In addition to their role in quality control, nanomaterials have also seen significant use in the preservation process. As food products are highly susceptible to contamination if not properly preserved, the packaging needs to have good thermal and mechanical properties. In many situations, materials are altered by introducing a nanomaterial coating of titanium oxide, silver, or silicone dioxide, endowing the material with antimicrobial properties, thus improving the product’s shelf life.20 Owing to the excellent antimicrobial activity and unique antimicrobial mechanism, silver nanoparticles (AgNPs) have been widely used in food preservation.21 Various research groups have employed different techniques to incorporate AgNPs into compatible materials. Fayaz et al. synthesized AgNPs through Trichoderma viride biosynthesis and incorporated the AgNPs into a sodium alginate thin film for fruit and vegetable preservation.22 In a similar study, Zhang et al. developed a novel AgNPs biosynthesis method based on Fatsia japonica leaf extracts for the preservation of citrus fruits.23 While AgNPs add significant value to food preservation, it is only one of the many available methods of improving the shelf life of food products. While natural essential oils are a valuable resource for food preservation, effectively utilizing this resource has been a challenge as the aroma from application of essential oils is undesirable. To circumvent this effect, nanomaterials can be used as a carrier to mask the scent of the essential oil-based preservatives, while also providing enhanced bioefficacy, bioavailability, stability, and functionality.24 One of the most recent developments in food preservation is nanostructured emulsions, which have been developed for the delivery of active ingredients and can be applied to the food preservation process to improve food safety.
Food packaging, as an integral component of the global food supply chain, protects food from physical damage, chemical contaminants, and microorganisms and helps maintain food quality and safety during transport and storage. As the food supply chain is becoming increasingly complex, there is an increased demand for innovative methods of packaging food items that allow for the monitoring of safety and quality throughout the process. Significant portions of modern food packaging involve the use of polymeric materials, which have been tailored to control the exposure of food to light, oxygen, and moisture. The ideal polymeric materials should possess the following benefits; they should be lightweight, economical, reusable, recyclable, and resistant to chemical and physical damage. Although traditional polymeric materials can fulfill many of these requirements, there are continuing efforts to maintain or improve packaging performance while decreasing the use of raw materials, minimizing waste generation, and lowering costs. For most existing packaging materials, the physicochemical properties can be improved through the incorporation of nanomaterials.26 Some examples of current research efforts into improving packaging material involve the introduction of oxygen scavengers and antimicrobials to form novel nanocomposites.27 In addition to improving food preservation, nanocomposites are also considered a more environmentally friendly solution to current packaging as they replace nonbiodegradable plastics.28 Moreover, to keep up with rapid globalization, the production of such materials extends the foods shelf life, allowing for a safer distribution of food products across longer distances.29 Additionally, nanomaterials have opened the door to potentially developing smart food packaging.30 While this field could have groundbreaking implications in the food industry, it is still in its infancy stages.31
Finally, nanotechnology and nanomaterials have been used in the removal of contaminants from production sources (including water, soil),32 inputs (including animal feed, pesticides, veterinary drugs, and fertilizer),33 and the final food product,34 through adsorption and/or degradation. In the past few years, many reports have demonstrated success with the removal of contaminants in water,35 fodder,36 and food products.37 One of the interesting methods reported in the recent years was focused on using nanocomposites to inhibit the production of mycotoxins in phytopathogenic fungi.38 While the inclusion of nanomaterials into packaging in the food industry provides many advantages, there are also risks associated with their introduction into the food production process. These include risks to human health,39 as well as livestock and the environment.40 Thus, it is essential that we fully understand the risks that accompany the use of nanotechnology and nanomaterials in the food industry.41
## 1.3. Emerging MOFs for Food Safety
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of hybrid porous materials that possess uniform structures. As a result of their high porosity and tunable physical and chemical properties,42−44 MOFs have been employed in a variety of applications, including gas45 and water storage, gas scavenging,47 contaminant removal,48,49 and catalysis.50 Due to their properties, MOFs represent a niche in the materials field, allowing them to be specifically tailored, which is highly valuable in the field of food safety. To date, MOFs have demonstrated their versatility and have been developed for use in removing contaminants from production sources, in production of food packaging, in improving preservation of food, and in the detection and monitoring of contaminants in food products. As research has delved deeper into understanding MOFs as a material, low toxicity MOFs have been developed as drug delivery vessels and tissue imaging agents. These materials have displayed good biocompatibility and have not had an adverse reaction in the host during testing.51 If MOFs become an integral part of the food industry, their toxicity and long-term health effects will have to be thoroughly investigated. While many people have approached MOFs as a material to be used in food safety, there has yet to be a comprehensive review on this subject.
In this review, the following subtopics will be covered: First, the design and synthesis and their effect on the properties of the MOF and how this applies to food safety will be presented.
We will also discuss how this can be used to improve quality control and safety monitoring. Following that, we will discuss the application of MOFs in the production process, including preservation, sanitation, and packaging. Lastly, we will discuss future perspectives, as well as potential opportunities and challenges faced by MOFs in this field. Readers are referred to some excellent reviews for the detailed chemistry of MOFs52 and some recent reviews for application of MOFs in analytical chemistry.53,54
## 2. SYNTHESIS AND POROSITY OF MOFS
An in-depth understanding of the mechanisms behind the synthesis of such materials plays a crucial role in tailoring structures with the desired chemical and physical properties. Over the last two decades, there have been many published studies on MOFs, encompassing various synthetic methods. An excellent review for a comprehensive introduction to MOF synthesis has been reported by Stock and Biswas in 2012.55 The review covers a multitude of methods for MOF synthesis, from the typical solvothermal/hydrothermal method to mechanochemical and sonochemical synthesis. In addition to synthetic routes, they also discussed the control of crystal size and morphology, the growth of MOF membranes and thin films, and the preparation of MOF-based composites. Additionally, Ren et al. published a review for pilot-scale production of $\mathbf{MOFs}^{56}$ which included representative MOFs such as MIL$101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ , HKUST-1, UiO-66, and MOF-5, among others. The methods used for the synthesis of these MOFs deviated from the typical solvothermal synthesis used at the lab scale. Of these alternate methods, water-reflux and steam-assisted conversion were emphasized due to their compatibility with batch synthesis. While synthesis is a crucial step in MOF production, we will be focusing on a select range of examples that pertain to the field of food safety, minimizing the use of incompatible solvents and reaction conditions. The effectiveness of MOFs synthesis is often determined by the resultant porosity of the MOF, with higher porosity indicating improved synthetic conditions. In addition to this, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns are also used as a reference point. However, although two synthesized batches of MOFs may produce identical PXRD patterns, their porosity can differ greatly.
MOF-5 is one of the most well-known MOFs, and it was first reported by Yaghi’s group in 1999.57 Single crystals of MOF-5 were obtained by a room temperature reaction of zinc(II) nitrate and $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{BDC}}$ $\mathrm{{'}H_{2}B D C}=1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid) in DMF $\left(\mathrm{DMF}=N,N\right.$ -dimethylformamide), with the gradual diffusion of triethylamine into the reaction mixture. Contrary to the initial hypothesis, the addition of hydrogen peroxide did not improve reaction yields. They later determined that solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DEF (DEF $\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}N,N.$ -diethylformamide) at elevated temperatures $(85-105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in a sealed vessel resulted in highly crystalline MOF-5 in a high yield $(90\%)$ . Following this, a series of isoreticular MOFs were synthesized by utilizing ditopic linear carboxylates that possess similar connectivity to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}_{\cdot}$ , such as 2-aminoterephthalic acid. The series was named IRMOFs where MOF-5 was IRMOF-1. In 2007, Kaye et al. re-examined the synthetic conditions of MOF-5 and found that a modification of the existing procedure yielded MOF-5 with a much higher surface area and hydrogen storage capacity.58 In this procedure, the reaction mixture of $\mathrm{Z}\mathrm{\bar{n}}\mathrm{(NO_{3})}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DEF was heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without stirring in the absence of air and moisture. The heating was stopped immediately following the cessation of crystal formation after about $^{10\mathrm{~h,~}}$ and cubic, colorless crystals of MOF-5 were obtained. It was also noted that prolonging the reaction time at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulted in yellow-tinted crystals with lower surface areas. This yellow tinting was attributed to unknown organic oligomers formed within the pores. These contaminants could not be easily removed from the structure, resulting in the loss of pore volume and surface area. Pore analysis of the framework following solvent evacuation after washes with anhydrous DMF and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ demonstrated a high Brunauer−Emmer−Teller (BET) surface area of $3800~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , a Langmuir surface area of $4400{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1},$ , and a high hydrogen storage capacity (7.1 wt $\%$ ) at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 40 bar.
Another good example is the synthesis of ZIF-8, which is also known as MAF-4.59 This MOF was first reported by Huang et al. and was obtained by a liquid-phase diffusion method at room temperature.60 Colorless, polyhedral ZIF-8 crystals were formed in an aqueous ammonia solution of $Z\mathrm{\dot{n}(O H)}_{2},$ that was carefully layered with a solution of Hmim $\left({\mathrm{Hmim}}\ =\ 2\right.$ -methylimidazole) in methanol. The room temperature prepared ZIF-8 sample showed the Langmuir and BET surface areas of 1400 and $1030{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1},$ respectively. In the same year, Park et al. reported that ZIF-8 could be readily synthesized using a solvothermal method using a solution of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Hmim in DMF heated at 140 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .61 After solvent exchange with methanol to remove the DMF and subsequent evacuation under vacuum, the resultant ZIF-8 showed a pore volume of $0.636~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , with Langmuir and BET surface areas of 1810 and $1630\textnormal{m}^{2}\textnormal{g}^{-1};$ , respectively. In addition to its high porosity, ZIF-8 is also known for its significant chemical stability. Testing demonstrated that ZIF-8 experienced no loss in crystallinity following treatments of $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in boiling benzene, methanol, water, or sodium hydroxide solution. Further study by Cravillon et al. demonstrated an effective method for the synthesis of nanocrystallites of ZIF-8 at room temperature using methanol solutions of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Hmim.62 No auxiliary stabilizing agents or stimulus was needed, but $\mathrm{Hmim}$ was used in an excess of 6 equiv with respect to $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ . The prepared ZIF-8 crystals had an average diameter of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and showed a micropore volume of $0.36\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a BET surface area of $962~\mathrm{m}^{2^{\bullet}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Most notably, ZIF-8 can be synthesized with water as the reaction solvent. Pan et al. found that ${\sim}85$ nm ZIF-8 nanocrystals could be readily obtained by mixing an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO_{3})}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with an aqueous solution of Hmim while stirring at room temperature.63 The reaction reached completion within $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~min~}}$ . Like the synthesis of ZIF-8 nanocrystals in methanol, the ligands were used in excess, in a $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ :Hmim ratio of 1:70. The micropore volume of the ZIF8 sample was about $0.31~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , and its BET and Langmuir surface areas were 1079 and $\bar{1173}\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. Shi et al. also reported an alternative synthetic method for ZIF-8, with water as the sole reaction solvent.64 They termed it a steam-assisted conversion method, where a solid mixture of 1:10 molar ratio $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Hmim was placed in a small Teflon cup within a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave. Water was placed in the bottom of the autoclave without contact with the solid mixture, and the sealed autoclave was placed at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Performing the same reaction without physically separating the solids from the liquid water produced an isomeric phase of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mim})_{2}$ with the dia topology, compared to the zeolitic SOD topology of ZIF-8.
The ZIF-8 sample obtained from the steam-assisted conversion method showed a BET surface area of $1470\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}}$ and a micropore volume of $0.69~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Although the two synthetic methods that utilize water as the sole solvent seem promising for scale-up production of ZIF-8, a significant drawback is the excessive amounts of the ligand, $\mathrm{Hmim}$ , that are required for these syntheses.
Interestingly, Lin et al. developed an efficient and environmentally friendly oxide/hydroxide-based solvent-free reaction method for the preparation of ZIF-8.65 While manual grinding of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and $\mathrm{Hmim}$ did not produce ZIF-8, heating of the ground mixtures at high temperatures $\left(100{-}200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ yielded highly crystalline and guest-free ZIF-8 (Figure 1). This method did not require an excess of Hmim. Moreover, as the prepared sample was guest-free, no activation was necessary prior to the evaluation of the porosity using ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption at $77~\mathrm{K},$ which gave a micropore volume of $\mathsf{\overline{{0.56}}}\mathsf{c m}^{\bar{3}}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ , and BET and Langmuir surface areas of 1450 and $1600\ \bar{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , respectively. This method looks promising for the production of both ZIF-8 in large scale and shaped samples of ZIF-8.
Although ZIF-8 can be synthesized in a large variety of synthetic conditions, many other MOFs can be challenging to reproduce, even while following literature methods as they are very sensitive to the reaction conditions. This can be especially difficult for those who are new to the field of MOFs. A wellknown MOF, MIL-101(Cr), first reported by Férey et al., is a good example of a MOF with a tricky synthetic method. According to the original report of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}\mathrm{1}01\mathrm{(Cr)},^{66}$ it was synthesized by a hydrothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $\mathrm{~\dot{~}1~m m o l})$ , $\mathrm{\dot{C}r(N O_{3})}_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{~\chi~mmol})$ , and hydrofluoric acid $\left(1\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ in $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(265\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ ) at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The MOF was obtained as a green powder, showing very high porosity with a pore volume of $2.0\:\mathrm{cm}^{3}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ , and BET and Langmuir surface areas of 4100 and $5900\ \mathrm{m}^{\mathrm{~\breve{2}~}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\nonumber$ , respectively. However, it should be noted that the same group had previously reported another MOF, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big),^{67}$ which was also synthesized from the same precursors as MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ . MIL-53(Cr) was synthesized from a hydrothermal reaction of a mixture of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ ${\displaystyle\vartheta{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , hydrofluoric acid (HF), and $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the molar ratio 1:1:1:280, which was heated at 493 K $(220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for 3 days. MIL-53(Cr) was obtained as a light purple powder. In contrast to MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , which has a rigid 3D framework with a 3D pore system, ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ has a highly flexible open 3D framework with 1D channels and a Langmuir surface area of about $1500\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The synthetic conditions of MIL-53(Cr) are very similar to those of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ , with the main difference being the duration of reaction, which was longer for ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ . This difference was pointed out by Hong et al. in a later publication, indicating that reaction duration and solute concentration were key factors in determining the reaction outcome.68 Under the given reaction conditions, crystalline MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ is formed during the first $^{16\mathrm{~h},}$ and ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ is produced if the reaction is not stopped after the initial $^{8\mathrm{{h}}}$ . In addition, the concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{\bar{NO}}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ affect the formation of products. Higher concentrations favor the formation of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big)$ over MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ . It was hypothesized that this is due to ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ only forming when nucleation is significantly slower. In a similar reaction, Serre et al. synthesized another MOF, MIL-88B $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ .69,70 MIL${88}\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Cr})$ is also highly flexible, but its framework structure is distinct from the structures of MIL- ${\mathfrak{s}}3(\mathrm{Cr})$ and $\mathbf{MIL-}101{\bigl(}\mathbf{Cr}{\bigr)}$ . Though no detailed description has been found regarding the synthesis of $\mathbf{MIL-88B(Cr)}$ , Carson et al. performed a study comparing MIL-88B(V), MIL-101(V), and MIL-47 (MIL-47 is isomorphous to ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ except for metal ions), 1 which revealed that MIL-88B(V) was formed under more acidic conditions than MIL-101(V), and MIL-101(V) was the kinetically favored product. In addition, when the reaction temperature was raised, the most thermodynamically favored phase, MIL-47, was formed (Figure 2).
Besides reaction temperature, duration of reaction, and concentration of starting materials, the feeding order of the starting materials to the final reaction mixture can also affect the reaction outcome. For example, Zhang et al. synthesized MAF-5, a zinc 2-ethylimidazolate $\left[\mathsf{Z n}(\bar{\mathrm{eim}})_{2}\right]$ , ${\mathrm{Heim~}}=\ 2.$ - ethylimidazole) with the zeolitic ANA topology, by adding a methanol solution of Heim, containing benzene or cyclohexane as a template, into an aqueous ammonia solution of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . However, a highly porous isomer of MAF-5, MAF-6, with the zeolitic RHO topology was obtained by adding the aqueous ammonia solution of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ to the premixed methanol solution of Heim with benzene or cyclohexane as a template.72,73 MAF-6 has a higher porosity than MAF-5, showing a pore volume of $0.63\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a Langmuir surface area of $\mathbf{\bar{1695}\ m^{2}\ g^{-1}}$ .
In spite of the previously raised examples, it is important to note that not all MOFs have sensitive synthetic conditions. UiO-66 is an example of a well-known MOF which can be readily synthesized from various conditions. Lillerud et al. first reported the synthesis of UiO-66 in 2008, which garnered much interest due to its high porosity, combined with the high chemical, thermal, and mechanical stability. It was obtained by a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DMF at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The as-prepared sample of UiO-66 consisted of intergrown cubic crystals of ca. $1-2\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ . It was determined that UiO-66 could be consistently reproduced as it was the thermodynamically favored product. Crystalline UiO-66 samples could be obtained even if the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC/ZrCl}_{4}$ ratio changed from 0.5 to 3, or concentrations of the reactants were tripled. A family of isoreticular MOFs of UiO-66, UiO-66-X (consisting of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}2,\mathrm{5}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ UiO- $66\mathrm{-NO}_{2},$ UiO-66-Br, UiO $^{1-66-2,5-\mathrm{Br}_{2},}$ UiO-66-1,4-Naph, UiO-66-F, UiO- $^{-66-2,5-\mathrm{F}_{2},}$ UiO- $66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{F}_{4},$ UiO-66-Cl, $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}2,5{\cdot}\mathrm{Cl}_{2},$ UiO66-I, UiO-66- $\mathrm{CH}_{3},$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}2,5{-}\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2},$ ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{CF}}_{3},$ UiO-66- $2,5\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2},$ UiO-66-OH, UiO-66-2,5- $\left(\operatorname{OH}\right)_{2},$ $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{OCH}_{3},$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}2,5{-}\big(\mathrm{OMe}\big)_{2}.$ , UiO-66-COOH, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}2,5{-}$ $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2},$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOH)_{4},}$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H},$ and UiO-66- $2,5\mathrm{-}\big(\mathrm{OCH}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3}\big)_{2}$ ) have been successfully synthesized by reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ or $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and the functionalized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-X}$ ligand under similar reaction conditions.75−86 In a separate study, Schaate et al. found that the introduction of a modulating agent, such as benzoic or acetic acid, to the synthesis of UiO-66 and its derivatives could significantly improve the crystallinity of products and the reproducibility of reactions, facilitating the formation of large isolated crystals with well-defined morphology.87 Following that, Wu et al. reported that highly defective UiO-66 (missing-linker defects) could be obtained by controlling the amount of acetic acid modulator in the reaction mixture and varying the reaction duration. The defective UiO-66 samples showed pore volumes in the range of $0.44{-}1.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and BET surface areas in the range of $\mathrm{\dot{1}000{-}1600\ m^{2}\ g^{-1}}$ g−1.88 Katz et al. found that the introduction of a concentrated solution of HCl into the reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DMF at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ accelerated the formation of UiO-66 at lower temperatures and resulted in shorter reaction times.77 Additionally, the synthesis was successfully scaled up 50-fold. The BET of the prepared sample of UiO-66 was compared against a theoretical plot, demonstrating that the synthesized MOF was highly defective. The BET surface area of defect-free UiO-66 (ideal 3D 12- connected framework) was calculated to be $1100{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}.$ , but the experimentally observed BET surface area of the UiO-66 sample produced by a HCl-assisted method was $\ 1580\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ which is much closer to the calculated BET surface area of a defective UiO-66 with a 3D 8-connected framework ( $\cdot1400\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ . The same group also reported a room temperature synthetic method for UiO-66.89 Instead of directly using $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ as the source of $\mathrm{zr}(\mathrm{\mathbf{N}})$ , a $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ precursor solution was used, which was prepared by heating a $70\%$ zirconium propoxide solution in a mixture of 1-propanol, DMF, and acetic acid at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Highly crystalline UiO-66 was obtained by stirring the obtained $\mathrm{{Zr}(I V)}$ precursor solution with $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ at room temperature. The room temperature prepared UiO-66 sample showed a BET surface area of 1450 $\mathbf{\hat{m}}^{2}\mathbf{\hat{g}}^{-1}$ . In addition, it was also reported that UiO-66 could be prepared via a modulated hydrothermal reaction, using a solution of $\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}$ and $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{BDC}}$ in a mixture of water and acetic acid ${(3{:}2\ \mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}}$ ratio) and refluxing for $24~\mathrm{h.}^{85}$ All these works suggest the facile synthesis of UiO-66. However, Guillerm et al. reported that the reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DMF at high temperatures up to $220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ also produced UiO-66, but relatively high concentrations of the reactants under the same condition could result in the formation of another phase, MIL-140A,90 which is a 3D framework with 1D channels in size of ${\sim}3.2$ Å and a BET surface area of 415 m2 g−1.
Overall, there are many aspects in the synthesis of MOFs that affect their yield, crystal size and morphology, crystallinity, appearance of defects, and porosity. These factors affect the performance of MOFs in various applications, including gas storage, separation, adsorption, and catalysis, among others.
Although the synthesis of MOFs through coordination of organic ligands and metal clusters seems trivial, obtaining the desired structure can be quite challenging. Table 1 lists the synthetic conditions for selected MOFs. These listed MOFs have one or more of the following key features: (1) high pore volume and surface area; (2) commonly available starting materials, especially for their ligands; (3) good thermal and/or hydrolytic stability. These features make these MOFs extremely attractive to researchers working in multidisciplinary fields, including studies related to food safety. For example, the MOFs with high porosity and high stability would be promising materials for food sample preparation and the separation of target analytes in the processes of food safety analyses. In addition, stimuli responsive and low-toxic MOFs are also highly appealing for the studies related to food safety. For example, sensing devices for food safety could be fabricated with MOFs that demonstrate an optical response to stimuli; non- or low-toxic MOFs could be employed in food preservation, food packaging, and food cleaning. There are also many excellent published works detailing the synthesis of specifically microstructured MOFs or MOF-based materials, including MOF films and membranes, hierarchical-pore MOFs,91−93 core−shell MOFs,94,95 nanoparticle@MOF core−shell composites,96,97 yolk−shell nanoparticle@MOF composites,98 MOF capsules,99 and carbon-based materials.100 While these works have significantly contributed to the field, their contents are beyond the scope of this review.
## 3. MOFS FOR FOOD ANALYSIS
Food analysis plays a crucial role in the monitoring of food quality and ensuring food safety.119 In recent years, we have seen a surge in novel analytical techniques used in the determination of food quality and safety. With this, there has been a parallel leap in the field of functional nanomaterials to keep up with the new technologies. Variations on nanomaterials have been explored in the search for improved selectivity, sensitivity, and stability. Silicon, carbon, and noble metal nanomaterials have all been thoroughly researched as a part of this endeavor. MOFs have attracted significant attention in this field due to their high porosity, structural diversity, and tailorability.120 In the following section, we will be covering prominent works in sample preparation, analyte separation, detection, and sensing in food analysis using MOFs and MOF-based materials.
It should be mentioned that water, or water containing medium, is commonly used in most of these processes. Thus, the possible effects of water on the performance of MOF materials should not be ignored. First, water could react with MOFs, leading to their structural degradation.121,122 Hydrolytically stable MOF materials are preferred when they are employed in food analyses. Second, some MOFs showing high adsorption, separation, or sensing performance to target analytes in organic solvents may lose their performance due to the competitive adsorption of water or the interference from water.123,124 Of course, the effect of water is not always negative. For example, some charged analytes, such as heavy metal ions, sometimes can be selectively adsorbed or detected by MOFs with non-neutral frameworks in water.125,126 Regardless, water is an important factor in the processes of food analysis.
## 3.1. Sample Preparation
Generally, more than $80\%$ of analysis time is spent on sample collection and preparation in food analysis, indicating that sample preparation is an essential step in the detection of target analytes.127 This is especially important when detecting low concentrations of analytes (trace or ultratrace amounts) in a complex food matrix. At concentrations as low as these, current analytical instruments are unable to achieve the required sensitivity or accuracy. In order to compensate for this drawback, it is necessary to improve sample preparation techniques so that interfering contaminants are removed and ideal detection levels can be obtained. Some examples of existing techniques include solid-phase extraction (SPE),128,129 magnetic SPE (MSPE),130 dispersive SPE (DSPE),131 solidphase microextraction (SPME),132,133 and stir-bar sorptive extraction (SBSE) (Figure 3).134 These methods have been widely used to extract, concentrate, and purify target analytes in complex food samples due to their high recovery rates, large enrichment factor, short operation time, low consumption of organic solvents, and the ease of automation. However, selecting the right adsorbent is essential to obtaining excellent enrichment and cleanup efficiency. Many functional materials have been applied as adsorbents in food sample preparation.135 In the past few years, MOF materials with microcrystals have attracted considerable attention in food analysis as convenient and functional sorbents (See Table 2). The molecular structures of typical analytes used in studies on food safety are provided in the Supporting Information.
It should be noted that many of the analytes in food analysis are large and many MOFs have relatively small pores, while the size of the pore windows of some MOFs makes it unlikely for the analytes to reach the pores, even taking into account structural flexibility and breathing effects. Yet, the MOFs still demonstrate the ability to extract these analytes, and this can be attributed to external surface adsorption.
3.1.1. Solid Phase Extraction. SPE has been demonstrated to be a powerful and efficient sample preparation technique, that uses solid chromatographic packing materials with a cartridge type device, to enrich and clean target analytes and eliminate complicated sample interferences.191 Due to the minimal use of solvents, as well as the ease of operation, SPE is often used for sample preparation in laboratories worldwide.
Undeniable progress has been made in developing new classes of materials that can be used in the trapping of various analytes resulting in the development of practical solutions to obtain high rates of recovery and enrichment, contributing to significant reduction of materials and solvents used in these processes.192 However, there is still room for improvement in SPE performance, which can be unlocked by exploring advanced functional adsorbents.
In exploring improved adsorbents for SPE in the enrichment and cleanup of trace analytes in food and water samples, MOFs or MOF-based functional materials have emerged as promising candidates. Following an initial study of a copper(II) isonicotinate in $200\check{6},^{193}$ interest in exploring water- or solvent-stable MOF materials for the development of efficient SPE in analytical science has increased significantly. For example, $\mathbf{MIL-}101{\bigl(}\mathbf{Cr}{\bigr)}$ , initially reported by Férey et al., is a hybrid super-tetrahedral structure, composed of terephthalate ligands and trimeric chromium octahedral clusters.66 This material possesses attractive features that are useful for the extraction of target analytes from an aqueous solution, such as high surface area (the apparent BET surface area: $4100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , large pore windows (12 Å for pentagonal windows, and $16\mathrm{\:\mathring{A}}\times\mathrm{1}4.\mathrm{\:\mathring{7}\:\mathring{A}}$ for hexagonal windows), large pores (29 and 34 $\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ , exposed activated metal sites, and excellent stability.194 Moreover, the existence of multifunctional groups in MIL$101{\big(}\mathrm{Cr}{\big)}$ allows for multiple noncovalent interactions with analytes, such as $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, hydrogen bonding, and hydrophobic interactions. Another frequently used MOF, ZIF-8 (also known as MAF-4), possesses a sodalite type $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mim})_{2}$ framework, and a highly hydrophobic pore system containing large cages $(\sim12.5\ \mathrm{\AA})$ interconnected by small windows (∼3.3 Å)60,61 exhibits high porosity (micropore volume: $0.636~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}~{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ and a large surface area (BET surface area: $1630\textnormal{m}^{2}\textnormal{g}^{-1},$ ) and has exceptional stability in organic solvents, water, and highly alkaline aqueous solutions, making it an attractive sorbent candidate in SPE.195,196
Another representative MOF, UiO-66, exhibits a relatively high surface area (the Langmuir surface area: $1187~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\dot{}\mathrm{~,~}$ with octahedral cages $(\sim11\ \bar{\mathrm{A}})$ and tetrahedral cages $(\sim8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ interconnected by open triangular windows $(5-7\ \mathrm{\check{A}})$ . UiO-66 possesses high thermal stability and excellent chemical stability in water and is even stable in highly acidic aqueous solutions.197 It was demonstrated that UiO-66 showed good adsorption capacity for polar analytes.188 To date, MOFs as SPE adsorbents have been successfully used in the determination of environmental contaminants,198,199 pesticide residues,20 0 veterinary residues,201 and other various samples.
Environmental contaminants, such as persistent organic pollutants (POPs) and heavy metals, can easily find their way into the food supply chain and have attracted increasing attention due to their toxicity and bioaccumulation. Many studies using MOFs in SPE to analyze these pollutants have been reported. For example, Jiao et al. used a copper(II) isonicotinate $[\mathrm{Cu(INA)}_{2}(\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]$ ( $\mathbf{\partial}\mathrm{INA}=$ isonicotinate) as a SPE adsorbent to aid in selective pressurized liquid extractions for the detection of trace residues of polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) in fatty food samples.164 The PCBs in pork, chicken, and clam meat were detected by GC-MS after enrichment and cleanup with $[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]$ . It was demonstrated that $\mathrm{\small{[Cu(INA)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}}$ exhibited higher cleanup capacity than some existing adsorbents, such as alumina, Florisil, silica gel, and Celatom, for the extraction of PCBs in fatty food. Although $\mathrm{\small{[Cu(INA)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}}$ is regarded as a MOF in this work, it is actually an isolated complex, which extends to a 3D supramolecular structure through intermolecular hydrogen bonding, which makes its high performance surprising.202,203 Unfortunately, the mechanism behind this result was not explored.
In a similar work, Ghani et al. applied MOF mixed-matrix disks (MOF-MMDs) in the automated flow-through SPE of phenols, a known environmental pollutant.168 The MOFMMDs were prepared using a method similar to MOF-based mixed-matrix membranes, by mixing mechanically stable polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF) and UiO-66 or UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (90 to $300\mathrm{nm}$ ) in DMF, resulting in MOF-MMDs with a thickness of ${\sim}0.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ . The performance of the MOF-MMDs for automated SPE of seven substituted phenolic compounds, 4-nitrophenol (4-NP), 2-chlorophenol (2-CP), 2,4-dinitrophenol (2,4-DNP), 2-nitrophenol (2-NP), $^{2,4}$ -dimethyl phenol (2,4-DMP), 4-chloro-3-methyl phenol (4-C-3MP), and 2,4- dichlorophenol (2,4-DCP), prior to HPLC analysis was evaluated using the sequential injection analysis technique. The best extraction efficiency was achieved using a MOFMMD synthesized with $90\ \mathrm{nm\UiO}{-}66{\-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals. Limits of detection (LODs) ranging from 0.1 to ${\mathrm{~\bar{0.2}~}}\mu\mathrm{g~\L^{-1}}$ were obtained through incorporation of the selected MOF-MMDs in the extraction process.
MOFs have also been used to enrich a trace amount of toxic heavy metal residues in food samples to enhance signals to obtain a reading in the detectable range. Wu et al. reported the determination of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ in tea and mushrooms with JUC62204 as a SPE sorbent. JUC-62 is synthesized from copper nitrate and $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -azobenzenetetracarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{{H_{4}A B T C}}\right)$ .205 The MOF showed an adsorption capacity of up to $836.7~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ with the adsorption equilibrium reaching the concentration of $0.1~\mathrm{{\mg}~\bar{\ m L^{-1}}}$ . The high adsorption capacity was attributed to the strong soft basesoft acid interactions between $\mathbf{N}$ atoms on the pore surface of JUC-62 and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions. Detection of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in black tea and mushroom samples with JUC-62 showed recoveries of $93.3\%$ and $90.0\%$ , respectively, when acetate buffer was used as the elution solvent.
In a series of similar experiments, Tokalıoglu et al. employed a highly porous $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOF (MOF-545) as a SPE adsorbent for the extraction of lead from cereal, beverage, and water samples,181 and Salarian et al. showed that a MOF $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{12}(\mathrm{MA})_{8}(\bar{\mathrm{mal}})_{6}{\cdot}18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right]_{n}$ $\mathbf{\hat{MA}}\mathbf{\Phi}=\mathbf{\Phi}$ melamine, $\mathrm{\:H_{2}m a l\:\:=\:\:}$ malonic acid) constructed by nanosized cuboctahedral $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}(\mathrm{MA})_{8}$ cages could selectively extract and concentrate trace amounts of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ ions from water samples.180 With ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) $(0.5~\mathrm{~M~})$ as the elution solvent, the use of $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{12}(\mathrm{MA})_{8}(\mathrm{mal})_{6}{\cdot}18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right]_{n}$ for SPE of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ ions resulted in satisfactory analytical performances. The system achieved an adsorption capacity of $120~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}{}_{;}$ , and an extraction efficiency of ${>}95\%$ was achieved. A low LOD for $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ , $500\ \mathrm{ng\L^{-1}}_{;}$ , could be reached with a wide linear range $(2-180~\mu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and lower relative standard deviation $\mathrm{^{\prime}R S D}<4\%$ ).
While the use of pesticides in agriculture has allowed us to significantly increase crop yields, pesticide residues pose a health hazard to consumers and are an essential part of food safety. Therefore, the development of effective SPE techniques for cleanup, extraction, and enrichment of agrichemical residues in complex food matrixes is essential. However, there are few reports on MOF-based SPE of pesticides. Recently, Huang et al. used MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ as an efficient sorbent in the microsolid-phase extraction ( $\mu$ -SPE) of five organochlorine pesticides $\left(\mathbf{\bar{O}C P s}\right)$ , including $\alpha{\cdot}1,2,3,4,5,6$ -hexachlorocyclohexane $(\alpha{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{HCH})$ , aldrin, $\alpha$ -chlordane, dieldrin, and 1,1-dichloro2,2-bis(P-chlorophenyl) ethane $\left(\mathsf{p},\mathsf{p}^{\prime}\mathrm{-DDD}\right)$ , in water samples.144 This was followed by GC-MS detection. Owing to the high porosity and adsorption capacity of MIL-101(Cr), satisfactory detection sensitivity for the OCPs was achieved when $4\:\mathrm{mg}$ of MIL-101 was used to extract target analytes from a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ sample solution. Similarly, Li et al. synthesized a MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{MIP}$ composite and utilized this composite in SPE of trace trichlorfon and monocrotophos in fruits.145 The improved adsorption capacity and selectivity of MIL-101 $\mathrm{(cr)}$ ${\boldsymbol{\ @}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{P}$ were attributed the high specific surface area of MIL$101{\big(}\mathbf{Cr}{\big)}$ and the good adsorption capacity of ${\bf M I L}@{\bf M I P}$ for trichlorfon $(47.6~\mathrm{\dot{m}g\ g^{-1}}$ at a concentration of $500\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}$ ). The LOD of the developed method was 0.011 and $0.015~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ for trichlorfon and monocrotophos detection, respectively. Two different of organophosphate pesticides in spiked apple and pear samples were extracted with MIL-101 $(\mathbf{Cr})\boldsymbol{\varpi}$ MIP and determined by using GC, giving good recoveries ranging from 86.5 to $91.7\%$ .
Veterinary residues are another high-risk factor that affects food with animal origins. However, detection of veterinary residues in animal products is difficult due to interference from protein and fat, which are typically present in samples at high concentrations. Yang et al. reported using ZIF-8 as a column packing sorbent in the online SPE coupled with HPLC for the detection of oxytetracycline (OTC), tetracycline (TC), and chlorotetracycline (CTC) in water and milk samples.137 In fact, OTC, TC, and CTC are often present in animal derived food products as they are broad-spectrum antibiotics that are used as medications for treating livestock and as additives in animal feed. Excellent analytical performances were achieved with the aid of ZIF-8, and the recoveries of tetracyclines in complicated milk and water samples ranged from $70.3\%$ to $107.4\%$ . However, the tetracyclines are large molecules (with 4 six-membered rings), which are too bulky to diffuse into the pores of ZIF-8. The observed SPE performance is likely a result of the adsorption of the analytes on ZIF-8’s external crystal surface.
Sulfanilamides (SAs) are a class of synthetic antimicrobials, which are hard to degrade but widely used in both clinical and veterinary medicine. In a recently published work, Dai et al. utilized MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ as the sorbent for SPE of four SAs, sulfadiazine (SDA), sulfamethazine (SMZ), sulfachloropyridazine (SCP), and sulfamethoxazole (SMX), in different water samples (drinking water, tap water, river water, and wastewater).136 In this work, the SPE performance of MIL-100(Fe) was also tested, and the recoveries of SAs using MIL-100(Fe) were lower than that of MIL-101(Cr). This was attributed to the larger specific surface area of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ . In addition, molecular modeling was used to explore the binding mode between the MOFs and SAs. The results from the simulation suggest that there are coordination bonds, intermolecular hydrogen bonds, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, hydrophobic effects, as well as van der Waals interactions between the MOFs and SAs. Due to their low toxicity, MOFs synthesized from natural products have attracted great attention in SPE. The CD-MOFs reported by Stoddart et al. are representative of this. These MOFs are obtained from reacting γ-cyclodextrin $\left(\gamma\mathrm{-CD}\right)$ , that has 24 hydroxy (OH) groups on both the primary and secondary faces, and Group IA and IIA metal cations.206 Li et al. reported the SPE of five SAs, sulfathiazole (STZ), sulfamethoxymidine (SMD), sulfamerazine (SMR), sulfadimethoxine (SDM), and sulfaquinoxaline (SQX), in meat samples (chicken, pork, liver, and fish)138 with CD-MOF- $1^{207}$ as the adsorbent, coupled with HPLC detection. The results indicated that the spiked recoveries of SAs in real meat samples were ranged from $76\%$ to $102\%$ with the lower RSD $\left(2.4\%-6.5\%\right)$ . The excellent adsorption performance of CD-MOF-1 for SAs was attributed to hydrogen bonding interactions between SAs and hydroxyl groups on the surface of CD-MOF-1 and inclusion interaction of cyclodextrin in CD-MOF-1. The results were validated by both static and kinetic adsorption experiments.
MOF-based materials have also demonstrated high performance as SPE adsorbents in the extraction and cleanup of the hazardous compounds in food processing. For example, advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) are a class of stable end-products formed through advanced Maillard reactions of proteins or lipids with sugars.208 AGEs accumulate in different tissues and organs in the human body and can be a factor in the development of many age-related diseases, such as diabetes, chronic kidney disease, and Alzheimer’s.209 To detect trace pyrraline, a representative AGE, in milk and milk powder samples, Liu et al. prepared a core−shell composite MIL-101 $@$ DMIP to be used as a SPE adsorbent. The materials were synthesized by coating MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ with dummy template molecularly imprinted polymer (DMIP), with pyrrolidine-3- carboxylic acid (PCC), a pyrraline analogue, as the dummy template (Figure 5).187 For comparison, nonmolecularly imprinted polymer (NIP) coated MIL-101 $\left(\mathbf{MIL-}101@\mathbf{NIP}\right)$ and DMIP without MOFs were also synthesized and the results revealed that MIL-101@DMIP outperformed the other two materials. In addition, MIL-101@DMIP showed specific recognition for pyrraline over three structurally similar compounds: $N^{\varepsilon}$ -carboxymethyl-lysine, 3-pyrroline, and pyrrole. This selectivity can be attributed to the cavities, which are tailored for interaction with PCC.
3.1.2. Magnetic Solid Phase Extraction. To further streamline the operation of SPE, magnetic solid-phase extraction (MSPE) has been developed for the cleaning and enrichment of target analytes in a complex food matrix. MSPE is known to be an effective and green sample preparation techniqu e,128,210 which provides straightforward operation and satisfactory performance in extraction, enrichment, and cleanup. In MSPE, the magnetic absorbent is directly dispersed into sample matrix. It can then be retrieved with the aid of an external magnetic field. As a result, certain steps that are present in a typical SPE, such as filtration and centrifugation, are eliminated. Another merit of MSPE is the high mass diffusion rates and transfer of target analytes between the sample and adsorbent phases. This is due to the increased surface contact between the sorbents and the analytes, which is beneficial for rapid equilibration. Currently, MSPE has been broadly applied into the preparation of environmental,211 biological,130 and food samples.189 An excellent review recently published by Cerda et al. has summarized the development and application of MSPE using MOF materials and the advantages of using said materials for sample preparation.212 One of the largest challenges faced in the production of effective MSPE adsorbents is the preparation of magnetic MOFs.213 Here, we focus on recent studies on improving preparation of MSPE sorbents for food safety.
Most published works on MSPE with MOFs or MOF-based materials focus on the extraction and enrichment of environmental pollutants. One notable example is the synthesis of hybrid magnetic MOF-5 by Li et al., which was then applied to the MSPE, followed by GC/MS and LC-MS/MS analysis of PAHs in soil, seaweed, and fish samples, as well as gibberellin acids (GAs) in mung bean, wheat, and buck wheat samples.162 The hybrid magnetic MOF-5 was prepared by introducing amino functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles into the solvothermal reaction for MOF-5. The surface amino functionalization was necessary to ensure incorporation of the nanoparticles into the hybrid material. It was demonstrated that the magnetic MOF-5 exhibited excellent enrichment capacity for both the nonpolar PAHs, such as naphthalene (NAP), acenaphthene (ANA), fluorene (FLU), phenanthrene (PHE), fluoranthene (FLA), and pyrene (PYR), and polar gibberellin acids, such as GA1, GA3, GA4, and GA7. Additionally, the magnetic MOF-5 was robust enough for cyclic utilization (100 extraction cycles) without significant decreases in extraction performance. It is important to note that these experiments were conducted in organic solvents as MOF-5 is highly sensitive to moisture.
In another work, Rocı ́o-Bautista et al. synthesized a hybrid magnetic material that was obtained by mixing HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles. The resultant material was used for magnetic-assisted dispersive microsolid-phase extraction of 8 heavy PAHs, benzo[a]anthracene (BaA), chrysene (CHR), benzo[b]fluoranthene (BbFL), benzo[k]fluoranthene (BkFL), benzo[a]pyrene (BaP), dibenz[a,h]anthracene (DahA), benzo[g,h,i]perylene (BghiP), and indeno[1,2,3-cd]pyrene (INP), in tap water, wastewaters, and fruit tea infusion samples.165 Similarly, Huo et al. reported a facile and environmentally friendly fabrication method for magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot100(\mathrm{Fe})$ and used it in MSPE for the removal of PAHs from water samples.166 The magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}100\big(\mathrm{Fe}\big)$ composite was obtained by in situ calcination of MIL-100(Fe) at $415~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen gas flow, where MIL-100(Fe) was partially oxidized, resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . The ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}@$ MIL-100(Fe) had a reduced porosity $\left(74.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ compared to pristine MIL-100(Fe), a result of the partial collapse of its framework during the pyrolysis to induce magnetism. Composite materials with a higher iron oxide to MOF ratio were similarly obtained at higher temperatures (500 and 700 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\backslash$ ). The composites were used for the MSPE of five PAHs (FLU, NAP, FLA, PHE, and PYR) from water samples in combination with GC. The resultant data had a wide linear range, high enrichment factor, and good reproducibility.
Chen et al. synthesized $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}(\overline{{\varrho}}M I L-100\big(F e\big)}$ core−shell magnetic microspheres for MSPE of polychlorinated biphenyls from environmental water samples when used in tandem with GC-MS.167 The Fe3O4@MIL-100(Fe) microspheres were synthesized via a stepwise assembly of MIL-100(Fe) on the surface of MAA-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles $\mathbf{\widehat{MAA}}=$ mercaptoacetic acid).214 The developed method exhibited good linearity within the range of $\mathsf{S}{-}4000\ \mathrm{ng\L}^{-1}$ and low relative standard deviations (RSDs). In a similar work, Zhou et al. prepared a magnetic composite ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\varpi{\mathrm{MOF}}{\cdot}5$ by introducing nanoscale silica-coated zerovalent iron $\left(\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\right)$ into the reaction for MOF-5.169 The prepared $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ was used for MSPE of five species of ${\mathrm{N}}{\mathrm{-}}$ and S-containing PAHs in water prior to detection by LC. The five PAHs used were Carbazole,
9-methylcarbazole, dibenzothiophene (DBT), 4-methyldibenzothiophene (4-MDBT), and 4,6-dimethyldibenzothiophene (4,6-DMDBT). The results demonstrated that the developed method showed good linearity for all the analytes in a wide concentration range, with low LODs and high recoveries. What is most surprising is that ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\varpi{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5 $ performed well in aqueous solution as MOF-5 is known to be unstable in water. However, this could be attributed to the fact that the target analytes were eluted from the $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ with $n$ -hexane. The authors also stated that the Fe@MOF-5 magnetic nanoparticles could be reused for multiple cycles after washing with $n$ -hexane and water. They reported less than $2\%$ loss in recovery after 10 cycles. Unfortunately, no PXRD patterns of $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ after MSPE were provided, and the stability of the composite was not further discussed.
In a separate example of MOF materials in MSPE used in tandem with flame atomic absorption spectrometry (AAS), Wang et al. synthesized a magnetic MOF-based composite, $\mathrm{SH-}\mathrm{\bar{Fe}_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ .182 The $\mathrm{SH-Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)}_{2}^{-}$ was obtained by treating $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ with dithioglycol solution. The composite, $\mathrm{SH-Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2},}$ exhibited high adsorption capacities and selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ due to the strong coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions and uncoordinated mercapto groups on the pore surface of the MOF. 1 M ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution was used as the eluent for desorbing $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions from the composite, and quantitative recovery could be achieved. However, upon studying the stability of the adsorbent in the eluent, it was noted that $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{B}\mathrm{^{\prime}C}\big)_{2}$ is highly sensitive to acidic aqueous solutions. It was also stated that satisfactory results were obtained when the developed method was used to determine the content of trace lead in food samples, such as rice, pig liver, tea leaves, and a certified reference material GBW10017 (milk powder).
Safari et al. developed a material, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{TMU}-8$ , to be used for the MSPE of heavy metal ions. It was prepared via a step-by-step assembly of TMU- $\cdot8^{215}$ on the surface of MAAfunctionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles. The material was then used in the detection of heavy metal ions $(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+},\mathrm{Cd}^{2+},\mathrm{Co}^{2+},$ ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ ) through MSPE of water samples (river water, tap water, and bottled mineral water), fruit samples (tomato, cucumbers, watermelon, carrot, apple, and onion) and tea prior to detection with inductively coupled plasma−atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES).216 It was demonstrated that TMU-8 had a higher adsorption capability for the metal ions than an isostructural MOF, TMU-9. This higher adsorption capacity was attributed to the presence of extra Lewis base type azine groups on the pore surface of TMU-8. 0.5 M ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution was used to desorb the heavy metal ions in $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{TMU}-8$ . However, the authors also mentioned that water stability is important for MOFs used in sample preparation techniques, since most extraction solvents for target analytes in food samples contain water.
Other works have been also reported on the MSPE of pesticide residues in food and water samples using MOFmaterials. Su et al. synthesized a core−shell $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@$ $\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite as an adsorbent for MSPE of triazole fungicides (triadimenol, hexaconazole, diniconazole, myclobutanil, and tebuconazole) in environmental water samples, followed by determination of the analytes using LC coupled with tandem mass spectrometry.147 Ma et al. prepared magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathrm{SiO}_{2}}$ -MIL-101 microspheres for the MSPE of pyrazole/pyrrole pesticides (flusilazole, fipronil, chlorfenapyr, and fenpyroximate) in water samples followed by highperformance liquid chromatography−diode array detector (HPLC-DAD) determination.148 The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ -MIL-101 microspheres were obtained by simply mixing MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ and ammonia treated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ in weakly acidic solution $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ =\ 5\right)$ subjected to ultrasonication. The electrostatic interactions between spherical $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ nanoparticles and MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ resulted in assembly of the nanoparticles on the external surface of $\mathrm{MIL-}101\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big)$ crystals. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}.$ - MIL-101 based MSPE method showed immense potential for the analysis of pyrazole/pyrrole pesticides in real water samples such as reservoir, river, and seawater. Lu et al. synthesized a magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L.101(C r)}$ composite and applied it as a sorbent for MSPE of trace pyrethroids in tea and environmental water samples when coupled with dispersive liquid− liquid microextraction based on the solidification of floating organic drop (DLLME-SFO).149 Although the analytical method combines two extraction techniques, the MOF-based composite was only involved in the first step of MSPE. Four target pyrethroid pesticides (bifenthrin, fenpropathrin, permethrin, and deltamethrin) in two environmental water samples and two tea samples were pretreated with the MSPE-DLLME-SFO method and quantified, resulting in satisfactory recoveries.
In comparison, less works on the MSPE of veterinary residues in food using MOF-based adsorbents have been reported. Recently, Xia et al. synthesized a magnetic, mesoporous core/shell structured composite, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{JUC}.$ 48, and employed it as a sorbent for MSPE in tandem with HPLC-DAD to determine the levels of five SAs, sulfadiazine (SDZ), STZ, SMR, SMZ, and sulfamethoxypyridazine (SMP), in pork, chicken, and shrimp samples.139 The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{JUC}{-48}$ composite was synthesized by growing JUC-48 on the surface of MAA-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles. JUC-48 was used for the study due to its mesoporosity and large 1D channels, which was hypothesized to be able to extract large SAs as a result of size compatibility.217 An excellent analytical performance was achieved with the LODs and LOQs of SAs being below $5.23\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $15.89\mathrm{~ng~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, and the recoveries ranging from $76.1\%$ to $102.6\%$ .
Some polymer additives and dyes can enter food products via the production chain and result in potential harm to humans. Wang et al. synthesized magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ via hydrothermal synthesis, resulting in particles with high product purity and good dispersibility.184 The composite was used for the enrichment of antioxidants and UV absorbers in polymer additives. An analytical method using MSPE followed by LC was established for the simultaneous determination of seven polymer additives (Cyanox 2246, Irganox 1035, Chimassorb 81, Irganox 1010, Irganox 1330, Tinuvin 328, and Tinuvin 326) in drinks and foods with plastic packaging. Zhou et al. prepared the mesoporous composite $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ PEI-MOF-5 using a two-step solvothermal approach. The resultant MOF was used as an adsorbent for the MSPE of two triphenylmethane dyes, malachite green (MG) and crystal violet (CV), in fish samples in combination with UHPLC-MS/MS detection.218 It was hypothesized that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{PEI-MOF-}}$ 5 was chemically stable as a result of the chemical bonds binding $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PEI}$ and MOF-5, resulting in a highly efficient enrichment and detection method for MG and CV. Shi et al. fabricated a magnetic composite $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{ML}\mathrm{-}101$ for the MSPE of six Sudan dyes (Para Red, Sudan $\mathrm{I-IV,}$ and Sudan Red 7B) in tomato sauce coupled followed by HPLCDAD detection (Figure 6).186 The composite was synthesized by introducing amine-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles into the reaction of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ . The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{ML}\mathrm{-}101$ based MSPE method shows good recoveries, low LOD, satisfactory RSD, and comparable extraction efficiency compared to existing adsorbents, including neutral alumina, ${\mathrm{C}}_{18},$ and HLB sorbents.
A recent work published by Wang et al. is an excellent example involving the fabrication of a postsynthetically modified magnetic ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}~\mathrm{SPs}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}\dot{8}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})$ .152 The material was then employed for the MSPE of domoic acid (DA) in seafood, followed by LC-MS/MS detection (Figure 7a). The satisfactory results of cleanup and enrichment have been achieved, and the recoveries of DA in seafood (included shell fish, cucumber fish, clay fish, and crab) ranged from $93.1\%$ to $102.3\%$ . The excellent performance of the material for DA is attributed to strong interactions between positively charged zinc sites on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $\mathrm{SPs}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ particles and the nitrogen atoms and negatively charged oxygen atoms of DA (Figure 7b). This was determined through the analysis of the recoveries of DA (Figure 7c), $\zeta$ potentials (Figure 7d), and XPS profiles (Figure 7e) of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{SPs}@\mathrm{ZIF}.$ $8/\breve{Z}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and reference materials.
MOFs have been identified as an ideal template for the fabrication of nanoporous carbons due to their diverse structures, permanent and uniform nanoscale porosities, and large surface areas.219 Nanoporous carbons (NPCs) have been prepared with a variety of MOFs, such as MOF-5, ZIF-8, FeMOF, and Al-PCP, as precursors.220 Recently, the potential applications of such NPCs as functional adsorbents in sample preparation have been explored. For example, Hao et al. prepared a magnetic Co-based NPC via direct carbonization of ZIF-67 at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .151 The Co-NPC showed a surface area of $315~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and exhibited super-paramagnetism. The material was applied in the MSPE of four neonicotinoid insecticides, imidacloprid (ICL), acetamiprid (ACT), thiacloprid (TCL), and thiamethoxam (TMX), in melon samples, followed by HPLC-UV detection, and a high adsorption capability for these analytes of the Co-NPC was observed. Similarly, Liu et al. prepared magnetic NPCs by carbonizing a bimetallic MOF (BMZIF) comprising ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a nitrogen flow. The BMZIF derived NPC showed magnetization saturation as well as a moderate surface area of $397~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . MSPE of 8 organochlorine pesticides $(\alpha\mathrm{-},\beta\mathrm{-},\gamma\mathrm{-},\delta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HCH},$ $\mathsf{p},\mathsf{p}^{\prime}\cdot\mathsf{D D E}$ , $\mathsf{p},\mathsf{p}^{\prime}\mathrm{-DDD},$ $\mathbf{o,p^{\prime}-D D T}$ , and $\mathsf{p},\mathsf{p}^{\prime}\mathrm{-DDT})$ with the prepared NPC as adsorbent, combining gas chromatography with triple quadrupole mass spectrometry $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{GC-QqQ})$ detection, was studied.153 The LODs and LOQs were ranging from 0.39 to $0.70\ \mathrm{~ng~L^{-1}}$ and from 1.45 to $2.0\ \mathrm{~ng~\check{L}^{-1}}.$ , respectively. Frizzarin et al. reported the application of submicrometric magnetic nanoporous carbons $\bar{(\mu\mathrm{MNPCs})}$ as MSPE sorbents in a sequential injection analysis (SIA) system with online spectrophotometric detection.175 The $\mu\mathrm{MNPCs}$ were packed in a microcolumn of the SIA system for the SPE of an anionic surfactant, sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS), in water samples, and the mobility of the particle was controlled by an actuated electromagnet, which minimized particle aggregation and consequently increased the extraction efficiency.
3.1.3. Dispersive Solid Phase Extraction. Dispersive solid phase extraction (DSPE) is a very powerful and attractive tool that is used in a variety of analytical applications including enrichment, cleanup, and extraction.13 DSPE was first introduced by Anastassiades et al. in 2003,221 and it has since been broadly applied into sample preparation as a simple and efficient cleanup and enrichment technique. Different functional materials can be applied as sorbents in DSPE to absorb a target analyte in complicated sample matrices. In the last few decades, the use of functional material, particularly nanomaterials, demonstrated major advantages over traditional sorbents due to their unique size and physicochemical properties.
Among them, MOF-based sorbents have attracted increasing attention in the development of DSPE for analytical applications. With outstanding stability and small particle size, MIL-101(Cr) is one of the MOFs that has been successfully applied into DSPE for use in the analysis of food samples. A recent study conducted by Cai et al. demonstrated the use of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ as an adsorbent for vortex assisted DSPE of amphenicols: chloramphenicol (CAP), thiamphenicol (TAP), florfenicol (FF), metabolites of FF, and FF amine (FFA), in aquaculture water followed with UHPLC-MS/MS detection.222 Li et al. applied $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ in the dispersive microsolid-phase extraction $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{D-}\mu\mathrm{-}S\mathrm{PE})$ of seven herbicides, fenuron, monuron, atraton, chlorotoluron, atrazine, ametryn, and terbuthylazine, in high-fat extracts of soybeans.146 Similar DSPE methods were also developed with MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ for the extraction of hormones201 and herbicides.223
However, the poor dispersibility of MOFs in aqueous solution limits their effective enrichment of DPSE target analytes.224 Improvements on the dispersibility of MOFs can be enhanced by combining them with other highly dispersible materials. Graphene oxide (GO) is an attractive candidate for this purpose. GO has many functional groups, including epoxy, hydroxyl, and carboxyl groups, which make it highly dispersible with long-term stability,2 and has the potential to form hydrogen bonds and have additional electrostatic interactions with target analytes, such as organic compounds or metal ions. It was also found that the introduction of GO in the preparation of MOF-based composites could largely enhance their adsorption capacity and stability in aqueous solutions.226 Recently, $\bar{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}101\bar{(}\mathrm{Cr}\bar{)}@\mathrm{GO}$ has been successfully applied to the extraction or adsorption of nitrogen- and sulfur-containing compounds227 and is also effective for a series of alkanes ( $\dot{n}-$ pentane to $n$ -octane).228 The $\mathrm{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{GO}$ composite is a promising adsorbent for the extraction of veterinary and pesticide residues in food samples. Jia et al. synthesized nanoscale $\mathrm{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{GO}$ via a solvothermal method140 and applied it as sorbent in the DSPE of 12 SAs, including $\scriptstyle\mathbf{SDZ},$ sulfapyridine (SPD), SMR, $S_{\mathbf{M}Z,\$ sulfamethizole (SMT), SMZ, sulfamonomethoxine (SMM), SCP, sulfadoxine (SDX), SMX, SQX, and sulfadimethoxine (SDMX), from milk samples. The $\mathrm{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{GO}$ composite was synthesized by dispersing GO in the solution containing MIL-101(Cr) precursors before the mixture was heated. MIL-10 $|(\mathrm{Cr})\ @\mathrm{GO}$ exhibited improved dispersibility and adsorption performance compared with MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ , MIL-100(Fe), activated carbon, and other sorbent materials used in pretreatment. Wang et al. synthesized $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big)@\mathrm{GO}$ using the same procedure and applied it in the dispersive microsolid-phase extraction $(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mu$ - SPE) of four residual drugs (metronidazole, $\mathbf{MNZ};$ tinidazole, TNZ; chloramphenicol, CAP; SMX) in chicken breasts, coupled with HPLC-MS/MS analysis.14 $\mathrm{\mathrm{^{-1}~M I L-101(C r)}}\ @\mathrm{GO}$ also exhibited significantly improved adsorption over GO and MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ in the D- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mu$ -SPE process.
3.1.4. Solid Phase Microextraction. SPME is known for being one of the most efficient preparation techniques applied prior to instrumental analysis. It was first explored by Arthur and Pawliszyn in $1990^{229^{\prime}}$ and has attracted great attention. SPME, a technique that can be integrated with extraction, concentration, and sampling, offers many advantages, including ease of operation, high efficiency, automation, reutilization, and low sample volume requirement compared to conventional extraction techniques.230 However, the performance of SPME is still limited by the fragility and limited adsorption capacity of SPME coating fibers.231
Owing to their excellent properties, such as high surface areas, tunable pore sizes, and tunable functionality, MOFs are promising candidates for improved performance in the application of SPME.232 Some examples of MOFs in SPME, such as MOF-199,233 ZIF-8,234 ZIF-90,235 MIL-53(Al),170 and MAF-X8,236 have been reported for enrichment and cleanup of diverse analytes.171,237 However, the fabrication of SPME fibers coated with MOFs or MOF-based materials is an important but challenging step. The general preparation strategy of MOF-based SPME coatings is via direct immobilization of MOF materials on the SPME support surface using adhesives such as epoxy glue238 or silicone.239 MOF-based SPME coatings have also been prepared via in situ covalent bonding235 and hydrothermal growth.233 Recently, there has been interest in developing robust SPME coatings bpaoslyedmeorn mMoOnoFlsitbhiyc nctoeagtriantgisn2g40MviOaFsdiirnectmaecrmobpeodrdoiunsg2o4r1ganidc layer-by-layer approaches.234 An alternative preparation method based on the sol−gel technique242 has been employed for the preparation of SPME fibers in the past few years; this method provides some excellent advantages, including easy preparation, maintaining the characteristics of the MOF, and robust adhesion to the support.243,244
MOF-based SPME has been broadly applied in the enrichment of volatile and semivolatile compounds in environmental and biological samples. In one study, Gao et al. used UiO-66 as a SPME adsorbent for the enrichment of ten PAHs followed with GC-MS detection.172 Similarly, Amanzadeh et al. coated three MOFs, TMU-4, TMU-5, and TMU-6, onto the surface of stainless steel wire245 for the headspace SPME of PAHs from water and soil samples. Saraji et al. synthesized an organic aerogel using iron-based MOF nanoparticles and used it as a SPME coating.246 Headspace SPME followed by GC-ECD was employed for the quantification of chlorobenzenes in the environmental samples. Ouyang et al. fabricated a MIL-101(Cr)-coated SPME fiber and applied it in the detection of volatile compounds (such as benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, m-xylene, and o-xylene) and semivolatile compounds (PAHs) in water samples.247 Zhao et al. prepared stainless steel coated with MIL-53(Al, Cr, or Fe) and used them in the SPME of 16 PAHs, NAP, acenaphthylene (ACPY), ACP, FLU, PHE, anthracene (ANT), FLU, PYR, BaA, CHR, BbFL, BkFL, BaP, INP, DahA, and BghiP in water samples.170 Bagheri et al. fabricated a novel nanocomposite coated SPME with TMU- $\cdot4^{248}$ and poly(ether sulfone) (PES).249 The TMU-4/PES coated fiber was used for headspace SPME of 4 organophosphorous pesticides, diazinon, fenitrothion, malathion, and chlorpyrifos, in water and soil samples.
Recently, MOF-based SPME has attracted more attention in food safety due to excellent performance in the enrichment and cleanup of analytes. Huang et al. used ZIF-8 as a SPME coating and developed in situ derivatization on SPME for the analysis of nonvolatile aliphatic diamines in fish.178 With the aid of ZIF-8 based SPME enrichment, the LODs for aliphatic diamines were below $33.2~\mu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and exhibited satisfactory recoveries $\left(78.6\mathrm{-}104\%\right)$ . Lan et al. developed a novel and stable SPME arrow with ZIF-8/PVC coating for the extraction and concentration of trimethylamine (TMA) and triethylamine (TEA) in salmon and mushroom samples prior to GC-MS detection.179 The reproducibility of extraction with ZIF-8/ PVC SPME was tested, and there was minimal degradation of performance $\mathrm{'RSD}<12.5\%\$ ) over at least 130 cycles. Zhang et al. prepared a high-surface-area ZIF-90 derived NPC (ZIF-90- NPC)155 which was then attached to a stainless steel wire by simple physical adhesion, forming a functional SPME coating. The developed SPME was used for the detection of pyrethroid pesticides, including bifenthrin and fenpropathrin, in fruit and vegetable samples. Lirio et al. prepared MIL-53-organic polymer monoliths as SPME coatings via microwave-assisted polymerization.143 The SPME sorbent was used for the extraction of penicillins (penicillin G, penicillin V, oxacillin, cloxacillin, dicloxacillin, nafcillin) in food samples. In addition, Lan et al. proposed a novel strategy to prepare thin MOFs coatings for SPME using cathodic electrodeposition. The resultant material was used for the analysis of trace amounts of four exogenous estrogens (including ethinyloestradiol, bisphenol $\mathbf{A},$ diethylstilbestrol, and hexoestrolum) in milk (Figure 8).159 Recoveries of the four estrogens were over
$60\%$ with RSDs ranging from $7.3\%$ to $18.1\%$ . More importantly, the developed SPME probe experienced minimal changes over cycles and could be used over 120 times without loss in performance. Li’s group fabricated a MOF-199/graphite oxide film and coated it on SPME fibers to extract and enrich organochlorine pesticides in complex samples.237 Zhang et al. coated MOF-5/graphene oxide hydrid composite on stainless steel fiber via covalent bonding for the SPME of triazole fungicides from fruit and vegetable samples.250 Li et al. presented a multilayer interparticle linking strategy for the preparation of MOF-199 modified SPME probes. The MOF199 coated SPME was utilized for the enrichment and extraction of ethylene in grape, wampee, blueberry, and durian husk samples via a noninvasive approach.251 As a result, the trace ethylene could be detected from fruit samples with the aid of MOF-199 coating SPME.
3.1.5. Stir-Bar Sorptive Extraction. Stir-bar sorptive extraction (SBSE) is an efficient, miniaturized, and environmentally friendly sample preparation technology, which was first proposed by Baltussenin in 1999.252 Since then, SBSE has attracted considerable interest in food analysis, environmental science, and bioassays.253 Generally, the SBSE extraction is done by introducing the SBSE device directly into the aqueous sample. While stirring, the bar adsorbs the analytes for extraction. The bar is removed from the sample solution, rinsed with deionized water or other solvents, and dried. After sorption, the compounds are desorbed in a liquid or in an analytical instrument inlet.254 Compared with SPME, the amount of coating materials required for SBSE is approximately 50−250 times higher, which can result in a significant increase in extraction capacity and improved recovery for target analytes in a complex sample matrix.255 In recent years, SBSE has been successfully applied to the enrichment and extraction of trace level target analytes in complex samples, such as environmental256 and food samples.257 In these cases, the functionalized coating on SBSE played a crucial role in efficient extraction of target analytes.258 At present, the available SBSE coatings are very limited and mainly include polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS), polyacrylate (PA), and polyethylene glycol (PEG). Therefore, the development of new functional coatings with good physicochemical stability and high adsorption capacities for target analytes is in high demand.
MOFs, with their tunable porosity, large specific surface area, and structural diversity, have attracted considerable attention and demonstrate significant potential for application in SBSE. Various synthetic approaches, such as sol−gel, magnetic adhesion, and electrochemistry, have been utilized to load MOFs on stir bars. Hu et al. prepared stir bars coated with PDMS/MOF-5, PDMS/MOF-199, and PDMS/IRMOF-3, respectively, via a sol−gel technique and used them in the SBSE of seven target estrogens, $17\cdot\beta$ -estradiol $\left(\operatorname{E}_{2}\right)$ , dienestrol (DES), diethylstilbestrol (DIS), estrone $\left(\operatorname{E}_{1}\right)$ , 4-t-octylphenol (4-t-OP), bisphenol A (BPA), and $17\alpha$ -ethynylestradiol (EE), followed by HPLC-UV detection.160 The results indicated that the stir bar coated with PDMS/IRMOF-3 resulted in the highest extraction and enrichment efficiency. Later, the same group prepared PDMS/Al-MIL- $S3\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ coated stir bars for the SBSE of 16 PAHs, NAP, ANA, ACPY, FLU, PHE, ANT, FLA, PYR, BaA, CHR, BbFL, BkFL, BaP, DahA, BghiP, and INP, in environmental water samples.173 The ultrasonicassisted liquid desorption (UALD) method was used prior to HPLC-FLD detection of the analytes, with acetonitrile as the desorption solvent. In a recently published work, they reported the SBSE of six organophosphorus pesticides (OPPs), phorate, diazinon, malathion, fenthion, quinalphos, and ethion, in environmental water samples by $\mathrm{PDMS/MIL\mathrm{-}101\mathrm{-}C r\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ coated stir bar.154 In these cases, the pristine stir bars used are capillary glass bars containing an iron wire. Wang et al. utilized a poly(ether ether ketone) (PEEK) jacketed metal bar as the pristine stir bar in SBSE. MIL-68 was grown on the PEEK surface in situ after the PEEK jacket was modified with benzoic acid groups (Figure 9). The prepared MIL-68-PEEK stir bar showed good robustness and was used for the SBSE of parabens (alkyl esters of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p~}}$ -hydroxybenzoic acid) in cosmetics and plasma of rabbits that had been exposed to cosmetic products.259
For the analysis of food samples, SBSE with MOF-based adsorbents is also a promising analytical method. Lin et al. evaluated the adsorption performance of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ , MOF${\mathfrak{s}}(\mathrm{Zn})$ , ZIF-8, and MOF-5(Fe) for 6 PCBs (Figure 10): $^{2,4,4^{\prime}}$ - trichlorobiphenyl (PCB28), $^{2,2^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetrachlorobiphenyl (PCB52), $2,2^{\prime},4,5,5^{\prime}$ -pentachlorobiphenyl (PCB101), $2,2^{\prime},3,4,4^{\prime}$ ,5-hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB138), $2,2^{\prime},4,4^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB153), and $2,2^{\prime},3,4,4^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -heptachlorobiphenyl (PCB180).174 The results indicated that MOF-5(Fe) had the highest adsorption capacities. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOF}{-}5(\mathrm{Fe})$ was then prepared by adding amino functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles into the reaction for MOF-5(Fe). The stir bar was prepared by modifying the Nd-Fe-B rod with the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -MOF5(Fe) composite. The MOF-5(Fe) based SBSE of the six PCBs in fish samples was studied. You et al. prepared a ZIF-8/ poly(methyl methacrylate-ethylene glycol dimethacrylate) (MMA-EGDMA) composite monolith on the stir bar through one-pot polymerization for the SBSE of five phytohormones, indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), salicylic acid (SA), 1-naphthalene acetic acid (NAA), 2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid (2,4-D), and $(\pm)$ abscisic acid (ABA), in apple and pear samples followed by HPLC-UV detection.163 It was found that the ZIF8/poly(MMA-EGDMA) based SBSE exhibited a higher extraction efficiency for the target phytohormones compared to neat poly(MMA-EGDMA) monolith and commercial PEG coated stir bars.
## 3.2. Chromatographic Separation of Analytes
3.2.1. Gas Chromatography. Gas chromatography (GC) is a powerful separation and analytical platform that is widely used in applications involving food analysis. Typical applications of GC in food safety are the quantitative and/or qualitative analysis of food compositions, natural products, food additives, flavor and aroma components, a variety of transformation products, and contaminants, such as pesticides, fumigants, environmental pollutants, and packaging materials.260 Currently, the most common stationary phases in GC columns are polysiloxanes, which are used for the separation and analysis of various target analytes.261
MOFs have received great attention for applications in GC due to their fascinating structures and intriguing performance in gas separation. Since MOF-508 was first used as a stationary phase for a packed column GC by Chen et al.,262 many other MOFs, such as MIL-47,263 MOF-5,264 and ZIF- $\cdot8,^{265}$ have also been studied for applications as the stationary phase in GC. The advantages of utilizing MOFs as stationary phases in GC have been demonstrated through many studies in recent years. Though MOF packed columns have been successfully employed in the separation of small molecules such as hydrocarbon compounds under high temperatures, it is essential to note that some larger molecules are difficult to separate on MOF packed columns due to the considerable diffusion resistance on bulky packing, which would result in poor resolution. Another disadvantage is that the packed columns for GC separation require kilogram-scale MOF samples, which can be challenging to prepare and typically result in high costs. To overcome these obstacles, Yan’s group fabricated a MIL-101(Cr) coated capillary column using a dynamic coating method, and xylenes and ethylbenzene were baseline separated on the MIL-101(Cr) coated capillary within $1.6\ \mathrm{min}$ without the need of complex temperature programming.266 Later, they developed a combination of SPME using ZIF-8 coated fibers, followed by GC separation with a ZIF-8 coated capillary for selective extraction and separation of $n$ - alkanes from complex matrixes such as petroleum-based fuel and biological fluids.234
For the separation of analytes in food analysis, researchers have paid much attention to developing high performance separation techniques with MOF modified capillary columns in GC. For example, Yan et al. successfully prepared nanosized IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-3 under room temperature conditions and utilized the nanoparticles as a coating for capillary columns by employing a dynamic coating approach.267 POPs including PCB-28, PCB-52, $^{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ -tetrachlorobiphenyl (PCB-77), $^{3,4,4^{\prime}}$ ,5-tetrachlorobiphenyl (PCB-81), PCB-101, $^{2,3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ - pentachlorobiphenyl (PCB-105), $^{2,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ ,5-pentachlorobiphenyl (PCB-118), $^{2,3,4,4^{\prime},}$ 5-pentachlorobiphenyl (PCB114), $^{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}},$ ,5-pentachlorobiphenyl (PCB-126), PCB-138, PCB-153, $2,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB-167), $2,3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}$ ,5-hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB-156), $2,3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime},5^{\prime}$ -hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB-157), and $3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -hexachlorobiphenyl (PCB-169), 10 PAHs [ANA, FLU, PHE, FLA, PYR, NAP, ANT, BaA, CHR, ${\mathrm{BbFL}},$ and BkFL], 4 polybrominated diphenylethers (PBDEs) [BDE-47, BDE-99, BDE-154, and BDE-153], and 4 hexachlorocyclohexanes (HCHs) $[\alpha\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HCH},$ $\beta$ -HCH, $\gamma{\mathrm{-HCH}}$ , and $\delta{\mathrm{-HCH}}]$ , which are common contaminants in food safety, could be efficiently separated with high resolution on IRMOF-coated GC capillary columns. The theoretical plate values of IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-3 coated capillary columns were 2293 and 2063 plates $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ , respectively, which were similar to that of commercial HP-5MS capillary columns, that possess a theoretical plate value of 2845 plates $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ . Tian et al. fabricated a MAF-5-coated sGC capillary column with 184 silicone elastomers through a dynamic coating method.268 The prepared GC capillary column based on MAF-5 was utilized for the efficient separation of 5 PAHs (NAP, ACPY, CHR, BaP, and BghiP), 5 OCPs (hexachlorobenzene (HCB), trans-chlordane, cis-chlordane, $^{o,p}$ -DDT, $p,p$ -DDT), and mirex. Additionally, Novachai et al. prepared a carboxyl-functionalized capillary using a precursor solution of [Cd(TCBT)] $(\mathrm{TCBT}~=~1,4,7,10.$ -tetrakis(4-carboxybenzyl)- 1,4,7,10-tetra azacyclododecane).269 The layer formed on the inner surface of the capillary contained individual crystalline domains of ${\sim}2.5\ \mu\mathrm{m},$ which was presumed to be an unknown Cd-TCBT coordination compound, as shown by energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) analysis. The Cd-TCBT MOF coated GC capillary column demonstrated good performance when separating 16 PAHs.
3.2.2. Liquid Chromatography. Liquid chromatography (LC) is one of the most commonly used and powerful techniques for the separation and analysis of multianalytes in food samples, especially when used in tandem with various analytical detectors. Stationary phases in LC columns play an essential role in the separation of target analytes. To improve the resolution of LC separation, many researchers have invested significant resources to develop more effective stationary phases, using various functional materials to achieve better separation of target analytes.
MOFs showed great promise for application as the stationary phase for in LC due to their uniform structure, porosity, ease of functionalization, and surface area. Thus far, MOFs have been employed as stationary phases in LC separation.270,271 One of the most notable examples is the MIL series, which have been intensively studied and tested as stationary phases for LC in the separation of substituted aromatic compounds272 and polar compounds273 among other common contaminants. Yang et al. prepared a LC column with MIL-53 (Al) via a slurry-packed approach.274 The MIL-53(Al) LC column was used for the separation of xylene, ethylbenzene, dichlorobenzene, chlorotoluene, and nitrophenol position isomers in the normal phase. The results suggest that the aforementioned compounds can be separated on the MIL-53(Al) LC column with good resolution. Recently, Stoddart et al. prepared CD-MOF with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and alkali metal salts and used them to pack a LC column.275 The CDMOF packed LC column could separate mixtures of benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, as well as regioisomers of xylene.
It is well-known that reversed-phase LC (RLC), where the mobile phase is more polar than the stationary phase, has a broader range of applications in analytical sciences. Based on results with MIL-53(Al), Liu et al. further explored the RLC separation of polar and nonpolar compounds.276 Subsequently, Fu et al. employed MIL-100(Fe) as the stationary phase to separate polar and nonpolar target analytes,277 which included benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, naphthalene, and 1-chloronaphthalene and aniline, acetanilide, 2-nitroaniline, and 1- naphthylamine. The separation behaviors of target analytes were examined under both normal phase and reversed phase. The results indicated that chloroaniline and toluidine isomers could be separated with high resolution using normal phase LC, whereas other target analytes were more effectively separated with RLC. The RSDs of retention time, peak area, peak height, and half peak width of the target analytes on MIL100(Fe) packed LC column were under $0.7\%$ , $3.6\%$ , $2.3\%$ , and $1.7\%$ , respectively. Of particular note was the excellent performance of MIL-100(Fe) as the stationary phase in both LC and RLC, which can be attributed to the synergistic interactions of hydrogen bonds, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, and coordination interactions between target analytes and MIL100(Fe). Chen et al. examined the chromatographic behaviors of divinylbenzene (DVB) and ethylvinylbenzene (EVB) isomers on separate LC columns packed with MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-100(Fe).278 The retention time of the target analytes on the MIL-100(Fe) packed LC column was shorter than on the MIL-54(Fe) packed LC column, but the selectivity of MIL$100\mathrm{(Fe)}$ for the DVB isomer was slightly lower. The varied chromatographic performance was attributed to the structure and pore volume of the above MOFs.
Because of the polydispersity of most MOFs particles combined with their instability in the presence of water and other aqueous solutions, LC columns packed with MOFs particles often result in high back pressure, poor column efficiency, and bad peak shape. To overcome these disadvantages, strategies involving the preparation of monodisperse MOF-based particles and monolithic columns are being explored in the hopes of improving column efficiency.279 Yan et al. prepared spherical silica-UiO-66 composites and then used them as the stationary phase for efficient separation of target analytes.280 The high column efficiencies for target analytes (including ethylbenzene, $p$ -chlorotoluene, $p$ -dichlorobenzene, and styrene) have been achieved using materials with theoretical plate numbers ranging from 5130 to 9990 plates $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ . Similarly, Zhang et al. employed uniform UiO- $66@$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ microspheres as the stationary phase in LC separation of xylenes and ethylbenzene.281 Recently, Qu et al. utilized a hybrid $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{d}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ -HKUST-1 material to pack a LC column. A high separation efficiency of 140,000 plates $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ was achieved with styrene as the model analyte, resulting from the unique selectivity of the HKUST-1 nanocrystals.282 Another strategy for overcoming previously mentioned disadvantages of MOF-based LC columns is by preparing monolithic columns. Yang et al. incorporated HKUST-1 into poly(glycidyl methacrylate-co-ethylene glycol dimethacrylate) to prepare a monolith.283 The HKUST-1 monolith LC column was used to separate small molecules and achieved high resolution and separation efficiency. Furthermore, in order to overcome MOF aggregation in in situ polymerization, they proposed a layer-bylayer self-assembly strategy for the preparation of the HKUST1 monolith.284 The separation efficiency of the final LC column with HKUST-1 as stationary phase for target analytes rivals that of commercially available C18 columns.
In recent years, MOF-based LC columns have been powerful technical tools that have exhibited significant advantages in separation of chiral compounds. Especially with the emergence of chiral MOFs,285 which show great promise as the stationary phase for the separation and preparation of active chiral compounds. The unique chiral selectivity of MOFs might be attributed to the combination of molecular sieving and adsorption effects of MOFs materials.286,287 Though MOF-based LC columns have been broadly employed in enantioseparation of a variety of compounds, such as racemic drugs,288 peptides,289 and other small molecules,290,291 to our knowledge, there have not been reports of using them for separation in the field of food safety. However, with the developments in design and preparation of functional MOFs, MOF-based LC columns possess substantial promise for application in the evaluation of active functional components in food.
Undoubtably, MOF-based LC columns provide a means to avoid the interferences within the sample matrices of target analytes and possess certain advantages in the analysis of hazardous substances in food. For example, Dai et al. synthesized uniform MOF modified $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ microspheres (ILI$01\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\textcircled{\cdot}$ in a cationic ionic liquid, with 1,3-bis(4- carboxybutyl) imidazolium bromide (ILI) as the ligand.292 The $\mathrm{ILI}{-}01@\mathrm{~\thinspace~}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ microspheres were employed as the stationary phase in a LC column, and the prepared column was successfully utilized for the detection of melamine in samples of baby formula. The results indicated that the ILI$01\varpi\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ packed column exhibited strong retention of melamine (retention time was about $4.75\mathrm{\min},$ ) and could eliminate interference from the matrix of baby formula. In addition, MOF-based LC columns have also been used to separate and detect endocrine-disruption chemicals, pesticides,293 and PAHs.294
3.2.3. Other Separation Methods. Compared with LC or GC, capillary electrochromatography (CEC) is a less common separation tool that possesses multiple advantages, including ease of miniaturization, excellent separation efficiency, high selectivity, and speed of analysis. It has also been employed for various food analysis applications.295 Similar to GC and LC, the stationary phase in CEC also plays a crucial role in achieving efficient separation. However, the lower loading capacity of the stationary phase in CEC often results in the loss of separation resolution.
MOFs with uniform structures, high porosities, and large surface areas present as good potential candidates for improved separation efficiency in the stationary phase for CEC. Recently, MOFs have been investigated as the stationary phase for opentubular columns in CEC (OT-CEC).296 Fabrication strategies for the preparation of MOF coated OT-CEC capillary column include physical coating297 and covalent bonding.29 8,299 In one example of this, Chen et al. coated HKUST-1 on the inner surface of the OT capillary column through epitaxy.300 The prepared MOF-based OT capillary was employed in OT-CEC for the efficient separation of substituted benzene, and the highest theoretical plate value achieved was $1.5\times10^{5}$ plates $\mathbf{m}^{\breve{-}1}$ for methylbenzene. Yan et al. prepared MOF modified capillaries by incorporating an Al(III)-based MOF, CAU-1, into poly(methyl methacrylate).301 The fabricated CAU-1 based capillary has been used as a column in CEC for the separation of basic sulfa drugs and structurally related peptides.
In a separate work, Lin et al. prepared MOF-based monoliths through the addition of varying amounts of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ in polymer and employed the monoliths as the stationary phase in CEC applications.302 MOF-based monoliths have an improved permeability, approximately 800-fold, compared to direct packing of the column with MOF particles.
Most importantly, OT-CEC with MOF-based materials as the stationary phase has been used in the analysis of various target analytes affecting food safety and exhibiting promising analytical performance. Pan et al. utilized a ZIF-8 coated capillary column to separate target analytes, including sensitive compounds (e.g., terbutaline), that affect food safety.303 Carrasco-Correa et al. incorporated ZIF-8 derived NPCs into methacrylate polymeric monoliths for CEC.304 Using these, PAHs could be effectively separated on the prepared capillary column with long retention time, minimizing the interference of sample matrices. A notable work conducted by Wang et al. demonstrated great progress in food analysis by using MOFbased OT-CEC.305 They fabricated an OT-CEC capillary column with the $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{cam})(\mathrm{bpy})]$ MOF via an amide coupling approach. The MOF-based OT-CEC possessed good theoretical plate values ranging from 43960 to 118185 plates $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ and could effectively separate 10 different sulfonamides using a $60\mathrm{\mM}$ phosphate buffer at $\mathrm{\pH}=6.8$ . Based on the efficient separation of MOF modified capillary columns, an analytical method for detection of sulfonamide compounds in milk has been developed. The results indicated that LODs and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{LOQs}}$ for sulfonamides were below 78.1 and $261.4\mu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1};$ , respectively. Though peak shifts of sulfonamides in milk and tap water could be observed due to the change in $\mathrm{\pH}$ value under the influence of the sample matrix, the separation capacity still remained satisfactory.
## 3.3. Detection of Analytes
3.3.1. Chemical Sensing. Chemical sensors have been broadly applied into various fields of application, such as food safety, health care, environmental monitoring, and chemical analysis as a result of their satisfactory sensitivity, easy operation, and rapid response.306 Given their sensitivity and precision, chemical sensors meet the increasing rigor required of food safety and food quality evaluation at critical control points of the food supply chain. However, the existing chemical sensors leave much to be desired in food safety, such as improved sensitivity and selectivity, faster responses, higher flux, improved robustness, and good reusability, that needs to be further addressed.
The emergence of new functional materials, such as noble metal nanomaterials, and carbon nanomaterials among others, provides an opportunity to enhance the performance of chemical sensors.307 Nanomaterials have recently been integrated into sensing systems for the detection of target analytes in food and other various sample matrices with greater sensitivity and reduced detection time. When applied in the development of sensors, nanomaterials are often integrated with selective recognition elements, e.g. antibodies, enzymes, and nucleic acids, to achieve satisfactory specificity.
Among emerging functional materials, MOFs have presented as a tunable and comprehensive platform.308 As such, they hold promise as multifunctional materials for the design and fabrication of chemical sensors,309 due to their tunable chemical and physical properties, structural diversity, and easy modification.
One particular subclass of MOFs, lanthanide MOFs (LnMOFs), have attracted more attention owing to their structural tenability and wide range of photofunctional applications.310,311 Ln-MOFs have developed into the largest class of luminescent MOFs312,313 and are often used as chemical sensors due to their unique optical properties of emission and excitation.314 Another advantage of Ln-MOFs is the elimination of background interference by time-resolved luminescence technology, a result of their long-lived luminescence. These advantages make Ln-MOFs suitable for fabrication of chemical sensors for use in the quantitative analysis of target analytes in complex samples.315,316
The chemical sensors based on MOFs rely on luminescent changes that occur with the adsorption and desorption of target analytes within the frameworks.317 The advantages of MOFs for chemical sensing include the following: (1) Organic ligands in MOFs often containing aromatic or conjugated $\pi$ moieties that could engender optical emission upon excitation with exposure to UV or visible light. (2) Metal elements, such as lanthanides or various metal nanoclusters, could also result in photoluminescence. (3) Intrinsic permanent porosity in MOFs further enable the adsorption of guest molecules, resulting in enhanced interactions between guest molecules and MOFs. (4) Exposed active sites of MOFs can be easily functionalized with various biological or other functional moieties. The various fabrication strategies, as well as the significant luminescence variation for target analytes, enable the luminescent MOFs to have broader applications in the field of food quality and safety monitoring.
As MOF materials provide a significant range of advantages, MOF-based sensors have been extensively explored.318 Till now, multiple series of luminescent MOF-based sensors have been developed, and their sensing targets include cations,319 anions,320,321 organic small molecules,322 gases,3 23−325 nitro explosives,326 and biomarkers.327,328 Recently, several excellent review papers have well summarized the advancement of chemical sensing based on MOFs.329 Generally, the chemical sensors based on MOFs can be categorized as follows: (1) luminescent molecules incorporated as ligands in rigid, porous MOFs for chemical sensing ,330 (2) lanthanide-functionalized MOFs for chemical sensing,331 and (3) incorporation of various functional elements into MOFs for chemical sensing.332 Since the initial foray into MOFs, many luminescent ligands have been developed and applied into the preparation of luminescent MOFs333 for sensing hazardous substances in food. In this review, we focus on the advancement of MOF-based chemical sensors in the detection of various types of analytes in food and water samples, to ensure food quality and safety (See Table 3).
Mycotoxins, a series of natural toxins produced by molds, have a significant effect on food safety. Mycotoxin contamination in feed and food can result in outbreaks of several diseases in livestock and can result in adverse health effects for humans.382 Therefore, the development of rapid and convenient sensing methods for detecting mycotoxins in human food and animal feed is essential to ensure food safety. Recently, Li et al. designed and synthesized a new luminescent MOF (LMOF-241) for the sensing and rapid detection of aflatoxin $\mathbf{B}_{1}$ $\left(\mathrm{AFB}_{1}\right)$ in corn.334 In their study, 1,1,2,2-tetrakis (4-(pyridin-4-yl)phenyl)ethane (tppe) was used as the luminescent ligand to synthesize LMOF-241. It was noted that the immobilization of the chromophore ligand in the LMOF-241 framework did not change the nature of ligandbased emission. $\mathrm{AFB}_{1}$ could quench the fluorescence of LMOF-241. $K_{\mathrm{SV}},$ the quenching efficiency, for $\mathbf{AFB}_{1}$ on LMOF-241 was $54227~\mathrm{\bar{M}}^{-1}$ , which was nearly twice that of aflatoxin $\mathbf{B}_{2}$ $\left(\operatorname{AFB}_{2}\right)$ $\left(32436\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}\right)$ . The result indicated that LMOF-241 possessed a high selectivity and specificity toward $\mathrm{AFB}_{1}$ . Based on the excellent performance of LMOF-241, a sensitive sensing method for the detection of aflatoxin $\mathbf{B}_{1}$ $(\mathrm{AFB}_{1})$ by fluorescence quenching had been developed and the LOD for $\mathrm{AFB}_{1}$ in corn was estimated to be $^{46}\mathrm{\ppb}$ using this method. It was believed that this was a rare example of a luminescent MOF with a fast response time and high sensitivity for sensing mycotoxins. Similarly, Li et al. employed a different MOF, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -CAU-24, to effectively detect aflatoxin B1 through fluorescence quenching in walnut and almond beverages and achieved improved sensitivity $\left(19.6\mathrm{\ppb}\right)$ .383 In addition, nonluminescent MOFs have also been applied in the fabrication of chemical sensors to monitor mycotoxins. Tian et al. proposed a novel sensor for the detection of 3- nitropropionic acid (3-NPA),336 which is a naturally occurring mycotoxin that is often found in moldy sugar cane. They took full advantage of the porosity of the MOF $\operatorname{\mathbb{\cdot}}[\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{L})])$ and the $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ sensitivity of fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) dye molecules to produce the target-sensing solid-state luminescent composite material $\big(\mathrm{FITC@\bar{[}C d(L)]}\big)$ . Based on the luminescent $\mathrm{FITC}\ @[\mathrm{Cd(L)}]$ materials, they realized sensitive sensing of 3-NPA with a quenching efficiency of $85.6\%$ for 3-NPA. Hu et al. presented a smart strategy to fabricate fluorescent sensors for the detection of ochratoxin A (OTA).335 They chose HKUST-1 as an adsorbent to prepare a $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/g–C_{3}N_{4}/}$ HKUST-1 composite, because HKUST-1 could improve the chemical stability of the composite and completely quench the fluorescence of the 5-carboxyfluorescein (FAM) dye through photoinduced electron transfer (PET). In the sensing system, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/g\mathrm{-}C_{3}N_{4}/H K U S T\mathrm{-}1}$ composite acted as the quencher, the adsorbent, and the separator. With the presence of OTA, the recognition aptamer could bind with OTA and release the corresponding FAM-labeled aptamer from the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/g\mathrm{-}C_{3}N_{4}/H K U S T\mathrm{-}1}$ composite, resulting in the gradual recovery of the fluorescent intensity in the sensing system. The developed fluorescent sensor with high sensitivity (LOD of $2.57~\mathrm{ng}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) has since been used to detect OTA in real corn samples.
Bacterial contamination in food not only results in food spoilage but can also result in many health-related incidents and long-term health issues.384 It is undeniable that sensing techniques for the rapid monitoring of bacterial contamination are one of the most convenient options for the control of bacterial exposure. Indirect detection of bacterial contamination in food by analysis of bacterial spores (e.g., dipicolinic acid (DPA)) has proven effective, and the quantitative analysis of DPA was of great practical significance for the detection of bacterial contamination, as well as the evaluation of the effectiveness of several food sterilization procedures.385 For instance, Rieter et al. developed a ratiometric luminescent MOF sensor for the rapid detection of DPA.337 In their study, an Eu-doped MOF named $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{BDC}=$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) was prepared and further functionalized with silylated Tb-EDTA monoamide (Tb-EDTM). In the ethanolic dispersion of Tb-EDTM, only Eu luminescence could be observed under excitation at $278\mathrm{nm}$ in the absence of DPA, as the Tb-EDTM moiety is nonemissive. In the presence of DPA, conjugation with Tb-EDTM to form the Tb-EDTMDPA complex produced Tb luminescence. Therefore, the Tb luminescence signal of Tb-EDTM provided a sensitive probe for DPA detection, while the Eu emission from the Tb-EDTM core acted as a noninterfering internal standard for calibration. The chemical sensor based on Tb-EDTM was sensitive to DPA concentrations with LODs of $47.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{~nM}}$ . Another LnMOF, $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}\mathrm{(FMA)}_{2}\mathrm{(OX)}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{)}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathbf{\widetilde{F}M A}=$ fumarate, OX $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ oxalate), was also employed in the detection of DPA via a “turn on” mechanism, and the LOD for DPA was $0.5~\mathrm{ppm}$ .338
In 2016, Zhang et al. introduced $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ cations into an anionic framework of bio-MOF-1 via ion exchange, resulting in Tb/Eu@bio-MOF-1, a compound with luminescent performance.339 The open sites of $\bar{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ in Tb/Eu@bio-MOF-1 could bind DPA and resulted in an efficient energy transfer from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , which led to fluorescence color change from orange-red to green. Based on the above mechanism, a sensitive and specific chemical sensor for detection of DPA was developed and the LOD of the chemical sensor for DPA was $34~\mathrm{{\nM}}$ . However, the limitations of the aforementioned sensors are the laborious and complicated preparation procedures of the sensory material, where high temperature was applied, and nonaqueous solvents were required for the MOF dispersal. Therefore, Bhardwaj et al. designed a water dispersible Tb-MOF, $\mathrm{Tb}\left(\mathrm{BTC}\right)\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{6}$ (BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid), and presented a high performance chemical sensor for the detection of DPA in aqueous medium.340 The sensing method possessed high sensitivity for the detection of DPA (LOD of $0.04~\mathrm{nM}\dot{},$ . In addition to detecting bacterial spores with the aid of MOFs, some chemical sensors based on MOF materials that allow for the direct detection of bacteria have been proposed. In another work, Bhardwaj et al. utilized IRMOF-3 for the detection of Staphylococcus arlettae (S. arlettae) by fluorescence quenching.341 However, a major issue with utilizing IRMOF-3 for bacterial detection is its low stability in aqueous solutions. Thus, they employed water-dispersible fluorescent MIL-53- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Fe})$ conjugated to a S. aureus-specific bacteriophage to fabricate a chemical sensor for S. aureus.342 The sensing performances of this developed sensor was satisfactory, and LODs as low as 31 CFU $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ( $\mathrm{CFU=}$ colony-forming units) could be achieved, with a large range of detection from 40 to 4 $\times~10^{8}~\mathrm{CFU~mL^{-1}}$ .
Veterinary medicines and pesticides play an important role in food production. However, veterinary and pesticide residues present in food and water pose a threat to food safety and human health. Therefore, exploring rapid response sensing methods for these residues are essential to ensure food safety. To date, there are some excellent examples of luminescent MOFs for the sensitive detection of pesticide residues in food. Tao et al. constructed a novel pillared-layered entangled luminescent MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{BP}\mathrm{\bar{y}T P E})]$ (bpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ biphenyl$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{BPyTPE}=}$ $(E)$ -1,2-diphenyl-1,2-bis(4- (pyridin-4-yl)phenyl)ethene).350 This MOF exhibited a strong blue-green fluorescent emission with a quantum yield of approximately $99\%$ . Subsequently, a MOF-based quantitative chemical sensor was established and applied for the detection of the pesticide $^{2,6}$ -dichloro-4-nitroaniline (DCN) in fruit. The chemical sensor for DCN could achieve LODs as low as $0.13\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . Similarly, Xu et al. exploited a highly luminescent MOF $\mathbf{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ZnPO-MOF}})$ with 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) benzene $\left(\mathrm{{H_{4}T C P B}}\right)$ as the ligand.351 It was worthy to note that the ZnPO-MOF possesses a high specific surface area (4073.9 $\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ and exhibited excellent adsorption ability and sensing performance toward parathion-methyl. Therefore, it could function as a sensitive and robust chemical sensor, using on fluorescence quenching of ${\mathrm{ZnPO-MOF}}$ to detect parathionmethyl. The LOD of the ZnPO-MOF-based sensor for parathion-methyl was $0.12~\mu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\left(0.456~\mathrm{nM}\right)$ , and the LOD meets the regulation requirement of the European Union’s Maximum Residue Levels (EU MRL) for parathion-methyl in food. Qian et al. synthesized MIP nanoparticles using a MIL101 as support for the fabrication of a quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) sensor for detection of metolcarb, an insecticide often found in pear juice.356 The selectivity of MIP was a result of the high specific area of MIL-101, which greatly improved the analytical performance of the QCM sensor, resulting in a LOD of $0.0\dot{6}89\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}$ .
For the rapid screening of veterinary residues, the stability of MOFs in aqueous solution is of significant concern due to the polarity and water-solubility of said residues. Zhu et al. reported a water-stable fluorescent MOF, FCS-1, chemical formula: $\{\mathrm{[Zn_{3}(}\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)(HL)-L(H_{2}O)_{3}]}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ ( $\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{3}\mathrm{L}=5$ -(4- carboxy-phenoxymethyl)-isophthalic acid).343 An important factor to note is that SMZ maintained high fluorescence quenching efficiency with FCS-1 MOF under a large range of $\mathrm{\pH}$ conditions $\mathrm{\'{pH}}=3.0\mathrm{-}9.0\right)$ . Han et al. developed a highly stable luminescent heterometallic MOF, CTGU-7, formulated with $[\mathrm{NaEu}_{2}(\mathrm{TATAB})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{3}]$ (OH) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TATAB}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}.\right.$ s-triazine-1,3,5-triyltri-m-aminobenzoic acid)349 which was used as a chemical sensor for the selective detection of oridazole antibiotics. Zhang et al. proposed a series of sensors based on Ln-MOFs for the detection using the anti-interfere ability of Ln-MOFs with antibiotics.386,387 First, they fabricated a chemical sensor for the detection of nitrofuran antibiotics (NFA) by filling a Ln-MOF, $\mathrm{\{[Tb_{2}(A I P)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{10}]}}.$ (AIP)· $4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\bigr\}_{n}$ (Tb-AIP, where AIP is 5-aminoisophthalate), into mixed matrix membranes (MMMs). The LODs for nitrofurantoin (NFT) and nitrofurazone (NFZ) were 0.30 and 0.35 $\mu\mathbf{M},$ , respectively. Subsequently, they modified a different LnMOF, $\mathbf{\bar{\{}}}\left[\mathbf{E}\mathbf{u}_{2}(\mathrm{{\dot{B}C A}})_{3}(\mathrm{{\dot{H_{2}O}}})(\mathrm{{\dot{D}M F}})_{3}]{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{{DMF}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{H_{2}O}}\right\}_{n}$ (Eu$\mathrm{BCA},$ in which BCA is $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -biquinoline- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate), on a stainless steel wire mesh to realize the sensitive sensing of NFA. The LODs for NFZ and NFT were estimated to be 0.21 and $0.16\mu\mathrm{M},$ respectively. They believed that the mechanisms utilized above could be integrated to provide portable chemical sensors for NFA and could be applied into monitoring of NFA contaminated water and food and to detect the abuse of antibiotics in livestock breeding. In a recent work, Wang et al. designed and synthesized two stable isostructural $\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})$ -based MOFs (BUT-12 and BUT-13) (Figure 11).116 The most notable discovery was that BUT-12 and BUT-13 had the highest $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values of $1.1~\times~10^{5}$ and $7.5\times10^{4}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for nitrofurazone (NZF) and $3.8\times10^{4}$ and $6.0\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for nitrofurantoin (NFT), respectively. The $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ results of BUT-12 and BUT-13 implied that their fluorescence could be efficiently quenched by trace amounts of NZF and NFT. Therefore, chemical sensors based on BUT-12 and BUT-13 would exhibit excellent sensing performances for NZF in water, with estimated LODs of 58 and $90\ \mathrm{ppb}$ , respectively. In another work, Li et al. applied amino functionalized MIL-53(Al) $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ MIL-53(Al)) for the development of a fluorescent sensing method for the detection of tetracyclines in milk.388 The richness of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ −COOH, and $-\mathrm{OH}$ functional groups on $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}.53(\mathrm{Al})$ endows the fluorescent sensor with high water stability, and they serve as bonding sites for tetracyclines. The results demonstrated the inner-filter effect, which resulted in the high quenching efficiency. The fluorescent sensor was used for detection of tetracyclines in real milk samples, with recoveries of $85.15\mathrm{-}112.13\%$ , with low RSDs $\left(n=3\right)$ ) of $0.03-$ $0.85\%$ .
Apart from luminescent MOFs that have been employed as chemical sensors, MOFs can also be used to fabricate sensors. Luan et al. presented a biomimetic enzyme-based colorimetric sensor for the detection of kanamycin in milk.345 In the chemical sensor, the MOF Fe-MIL- $\cdot88\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ acted as a signal tag, combining catalytic hairpin assembly (CHA)-assisted target recycling for signal amplification. The sensor exhibited good analytical performances, and the LOD for kanamycin in milk was $0.2\ \mathrm{pg}^{\mathrm{^{-}m L^{-1}}}$ . Additionally, there have been instances of utilizing two-dimensional (2D) MOFs as a quencher in sensing platforms for the detection of HIV DNA and thrombin,38 9 multiplexed DNA,390,391 $\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{N}_{1}$ antibodies,392 and adenosine triphosphate (ATP).393 Recently, Yang et al. utilized a new 2D MOF (Cu-TCPP nanosheets) to fabricate aptasensing platforms based on fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) for the sensitive detection of antibiotics (CAP) in milk and fish.344
Environmental pollutants can be introduced into food through carry-over from water and soil, which can result in adverse effects on the human health. Therefore, it is crucial that we develop sensitive and robust chemical sensors for monitoring environmental contaminants in food and water. Recently, Wang et al. developed Ln-MOF-1, with the chemical composition $\left\{\left[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{3}(\mathrm{D}\hat{\mathrm{MF}})_{2}\right]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{MeOH}\right\}_{n}$ .394 The 5- (4H-1,2,4-triazol-4-yl) benzene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\right)$ was chosen as ligand and combined with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to produce a 3,8-connected tfz-d net. An interesting discovery regarding LnMOF-1 was that the decrease of its luminescent intensity was related to the increase of the number of chlorine atoms on the benzene ring of polychlorinated benzenes. Based on this property, a novel chemical sensor for the detection of polychlorinated benzenes was testing, giving satisfactory analytical performances for 1,2,4-trichlorobenzene (1,2,4- TCB), 1,2,3,4-tetrachlorobenzene (1,2,3,4-TCB), 1,2,4,5-tetrachlorobenzene (1,2,4,5-TCB), pentachlorobenzene (PeCB), and hexachlorobenzene (HCB). In the case of 1,2,4-TCB, the luminescent intensity of Ln-MOF-1 was almost completely quenched at $100~\mathrm{mM}$ and a maximum quenching efficiency of $95\%$ could be achieved. Hao et al. designed luminescent Eufunctionalized MOFs as chemical sensors to detect 1- hydroxypyrene,358 a biomarker of PAH exposure in living organisms. The luminescence of Eu-functionalized MOFs can be effectively quenched by specific concentrations of 1- hydroxypyrene via a FRET pathway, allowing for the sensitive detection of 1-hydroxypyrene. Subsequently, the same research group developed a series of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ functionalized MOF sensors to detect the presence of food preservatives395 and metal ions.396
For the detection of metal ions, the selectivity and robustness of chemical sensors are still significant challenges. In order to improve the selectivity and specificity of MOFbased chemical sensors, some strategies have been developed, such as incorporation of unsaturated Lewis acid sites397 and immobilization of Lewis basic sites in MOFs.304 In one such study, Chen et al. designed a luminescent MOF with Lewis basic pyridyl sites for the sensing of metal ions398 and achieved satisfactory analytical performances. Lin et al. prepared BPEICQDs/ZIF-8 composites by encapsulating branched poly(ethylenimine)-capped carbon quantum dots (BPEI-CQDs) with a high FL quantum yield into the ZIF-8.366 The excellent adsorption capacity of ZIF-8 for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ amplified the sensing signal and specificity. The chemical sensor based on BPEICQDs/ZIF-8 exhibited good sensing performance for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in water, and LODs as low as $80~\mathrm{pM}$ could be achieved. Chen et al. constructed a 3D Co(II)-MOF with exposed Lewis base sites by using a pyrimidyl−biimidazole ligand399 and employed it as a selective chemical sensor for the detection of $\mathbb{A}^{\hat{3}+}$ with enhanced emission. $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ LODs as low as $0.7~\mathrm{\mM}$ were achieved with the Co(II)-MOF sensor, and the sensor survived five cycles with minimal degradation. $\mathtt{X u}$ et al. developed three Ln-BTB frameworks $\mathrm{{Ln}=\mathrm{{Eu}}}$ , Dy, Yb) which were used to carry out sensitive and selective detection of $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ .400 Lv et al. employed a $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(T P O M)(N H_{2}{\cdot}B D C)_{2}]}}$ · $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}N H_{2}{-}Z n{-}M O F}$ ), to develop a chemical sensor for detection of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ .401 Detection using $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -ZnMOF for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ in aqueous conditions was realized via fluorescence enhancement. Recently, Zhao et al. developed a family of thiophene-based MOFs, $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{L})$ (BBI)· $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\bar{_{2}}]$ and $[\mathrm{Cd}\dot{(\mathrm{L})}{-}(\mathrm{TP\bar{O}M})_{0.75}]{\cdot}x S$ and employed them in the detection of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ .402 Liu et al. utilized an Eu (III)-based MOF (Eu-MOF) in the detection of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in aqueous medium through luminescence quenching, achieving LODs of $<2.88$ ppb in samples of potable water.126 Additionally, many works utilizing MOF-based materials for the detection of ions, such as $\mathrm{Pb^{2+},^{403}\:\:\ U O_{2}^{2+},^{404}\:\:\:I^{-},^{405}}$ $\mathrm{NO^{2-},^{361}~F e^{3+406}}$ and $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+407}$ have been reported. Recently, Cho et al. proposed a smart design for a chemical sensor for the detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ using a MOF-based material.408 The ligand was designed with three important functional regions: one for coordination with the metal cluster, one for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ detection, and one that serves as the fluorophore (Figure 12).
Subsequently, they constructed coordination polymer particles (CPP-16) with a framework resembling that of MOF-5 by using the multifunctional ligand and $\mathsf{\bar{Z}n}^{2+}$ , establishing a selective fluorescence quenching chemical sensor for the detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ .
To further improve the selectivity of chemical sensors for the detection of metal ions, many strategies have been proposed. An example of this was explored by Wu et al., who developed a novel fluorescent sensor for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ detection in aqueous solutions.365 In this work, they used $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ specific ssDNA in tandem with a fluorescent FAM dye label as recognition elements and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as the quenching agent for the sensing of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ .
In addition to compounds that can be introduced to food in preprocessing stages, certain illegal additives found in the foods and the packaging can pose severe hazards to humans. One such example is melamine, which achieved worldwide recognition in 2008, as a toxin that caused severe health issues in children under the age of two, when it was found in powdered milk samples. Following this scandal, Lee et al. developed (AgL)-(riboflavin) chelation crystals for the detection of melamine by chelating riboflavin onto a silverbased MOF (AgL, $\mathrm{~L~}=\mathrm{~4~}$ -cyanobenzoate).369 The $(\mathrm{AgL})$ - (riboflavin) could produce visible light and be applied to the detection of melamine in milk. Similarly, Zhou et al. proposed a multiresponsive chemical sensor for the detection of fluoride and formaldehyde.357 In their research, two isostructural LnMOFs, $\left[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{BPDC})(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]_{n}$ $\mathit{\'{L n}}=\mathit{\ l}\mathrm{u}$ and Tb, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $^{=1,4}$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid), were developed and used as sensing substrates. Recently, a fluorescent biosensor for BPA was developed and the mechanism of action is based on the adsorption and quenching ability of a nanosized iron MOF $\left(\mathrm{Fe-}\mathrm{\bar{M}I L}{\cdot}88\mathrm{B}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}\right)$ for a fluorescence modified DNA aptamer.359 The introduction of a MOF material to the sensing method was a novel attempt to achieve the sensitive and specific detection of BPA with a biosensor. This method permitted the detection of BPA with a LOD of $4.1\times10^{-14}\mathrm{M},$ which is comparable to the LODs of chromatographic methods and enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA).
Water is an active molecule that interacts with compounds in food items which often results in food spoilage, and thus, food safety. As such, it is essential to have a rapid sensing device to monitor and control water levels at various points in the processes of the food industry. Recently, Ohria et al. developed a rapid responsive water sensor for monitoring trace water levels.409 This sensor consisted of a Cu-BTC film as a sensing material and had a LOD of $^{40}\mathrm{\ppb}$ . Upon water adsorption, the color of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC in the sensor was observed to have changed in both depth and tone. In 2017, Chen et al. developed a Zn-MOF for use as a quick, convenient, and selective chemical sensor for water analysis.410 In their study, a robust microporous ${\mathrm{Zn-MOF}}.$ , $\mathrm{Zn(hpi_{2}c f)}$ (DMF) $\left(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}\right)$ , was assembled with a dual-emissive ligand. The $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF exhibited a characteristic excited state intramolecular proton transfer and proved to be an effective sensing material for trace amounts of water and was sensitive down to a molecular level. Furthermore, to make the system more convenient to operate, paper-based sensing devices were constructed and applied into humidity sensing $\mathrm{^{\prime}R H}<1\%)$ and detection of trace water $\left(<0.05\%\mathrm{\blacktriangledown}\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ in various applications, including food inspection.
Gas sensors are widely used in environmental monitoring but are also often employed in applications for the assessment of food quality and safety. The measurement of gases, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and $S\mathrm{O}_{2},$ is very important for evaluating leaks in food packaging, 411 ventilation in storage facilities ,412 and extent of food spoilage.413 A sensor for the detection of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has been developed via coating optical fibers with MOF (HKUST-1).414 Layer-by-layer techniques used in the preparation of HKUST-1 thin films were highly effective, and the films that contained a minimum of 40 layers exhibited satisfactory sensing performance toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with LODs as low as 401 ppm.
In addition to MOF modified thin films, luminescent MOFs that trigger a “turn-on” mechanism have been developed and applied as sensors in the field of food safety.415,416 An example of this was demonstrated by Shustova et al., who reported selective “turn-on” sensors for the detection of ammonia by high-temperature fluorescence in two different MOFs with open metal sites.330 In their study, both $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{(TCPE)}$ $\mathrm{(TCPE=}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) ethylene) and $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{DHBDC})$ $\left(\mathrm{DHBDC}^{2-}\ =\ 2,\dot{S}\right.$ -dihydroxybenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate) functioned as sensing materials. Similarly, Wang et al. employed luminescent MOF- ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ as sensing materials to detect $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gas and its derivatives by a “turn on” mechanism.375 With the MOF as the sensing material, a lower LOD of $0.168\mathrm{ppm}$ and a rapid response times of less than 15s could be achieved for sensing $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $S O_{3}{}^{2-}$ . Importantly, the luminescence turn-on phenomenon could be observed, and signal read out could be realized only with naked eyes. Cao et al. proposed a novel turnon mechanism and developed a chemical sensing probe based on $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{{III}}}{-}M I L{-}}88{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for $\mathrm{\hat{H}}_{2}S$ .374 The sensing strategy involves the collapse of MOF structures in the presence of target analytes, and release of the fluorescent ligand resulted in increase in fluorescence intensity. The resultant sensor was sensitive to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ with a LOD ${\mathsf{c a.l0\ }}\mu{\mathrm{M}}$ .
MOF-based sensors have also been employed in the evaluation and control of food quality. A MOF composite, methyl red@lanthanide MOFs $\begin{array}{r}{\big(\mathrm{MR}@\mathrm{EuMOFs}\big)}\end{array}$ , was synthesized by ${\mathrm{Xu}}_{} $ et al. and was used in the construction of fluorescent chemical sensors to detect indicators of food spoilage.376 Utilizing the characteristic double-stimuli-responsive fluorescence of $\bar{\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and MR in MR@EuMOFs, a portable chemical sensing system was developed to detect histamine (HI) for evaluating food spoilage. In the logic gate based sensing system, the normalized fluorescence signals of MR $\left(\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{MR}}\right)$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ $({\mathrm{F_{Eu}}})$ function as the binary outputs, with a threshold value of 0.5 (Figure 13a, b). Furthermore, five predetermined concentrations of HI in a range from 0 to $100\times$ $\overline{{10^{-6}}}\mathbf{M}$ were set as the input signals. The output signals of the chemical sensor for HI were normalized (Figure 13c). When concentrations of HI were $40\times10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ and below, the NULL response was triggered for $\mathrm{F_{MR}},$ while concentrations of $80\times$ $10^{\overset{\cdot}{-}6}\mathrm{M}$ and above triggered a UNITY response (Figure 13d). A similar response was detected with $\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{E}\upnu}$ with UNITY at concentrations of $20\times10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ and below, and with NULL at $40\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ and above. The various combinations of binary output from both $\mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{MR}}$ and $\mathrm{F_{Eu}}$ were charted at each concentration, and the resultant colorimetric response was used to detect levels of HI and evaluate food spoilage. What is most interesting about this strategy is that a dual-response system was applied, with the individual parts working in tandem to allow for a sliding scale of detection, giving a more accurate readout. A more complex system using similar principles was developed by Lee et al., who constructed a “biomimetic tongue” by integrating $\left[\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{OH})({\mathrm{bdc}})\right]_{n},$ [Tb${\bigl(}\b{\mathrm{btc}}{\bigr)}\bigr]_{n}$ (MOF-76), and $\mathrm{\small{[Ca-3(btc)_{2}(D M F)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}3H_{2}O}}$ to distinguish aqueous solutions that contain compounds indicative of the five basic taste groups: sucrose (sweet), caffeine (bitter), citric acid (sour), sodium chloride (salty), and monosodium glutamate (umami).417 This integrated sensing device demonstrated great potential for application in the food industry with further development.
3.3.2. Electrochemical Sensing. As most MOFs are considered insulating materials, they were not viewed as viable candidates for conductivity-based applications. Recently, several MOFs have demonstrated excellent electrical conductivity and high charge mobility.418 More importantly, many MOFs have also been found to participate in redox events that are useful in electrochemical analysis.419 In the past few years, many MOF-based electrochemical sensors have demonstrated high analytical performances in the detection of biomolecules,420,421 gases,422 and pharmaceuticals.423
Generally, MOFs possess certain features that make them attractive candidates for improving the performance of electrochemical sensors. First, the flexible porous structures of MOFs allow guest molecules to diffuse into their pores, which can be tailored for shape- or size-selectivity.424 Second, MOFs provide an effective scaffold for the encapsulation of other species, such as nanoparticles, proteins, or enzymes,425 improving electrochemical signals and increasing the stability of biomolecules.426,427 T hird, the availability of pendent functional groups on the MOFs allows them to be modified with functional materials such as aptamers, antibodies, or MIPs, which can improve the selectivity of the resulting electrochemical sensor s.428,429 Taking those attributes into consideration, it is clear that MOFs offer unique potential as a sensing platform in food safety. In this section, recent advancements on MOF-based electrochemical sensors in food detection and related studies are reviewed.
MOF-based electrochemical sensors have been successfully applied for the detection of veterinary residues in food and water samples. Liu et al. demonstrated that two unique sensors, made from Al-based MOFs (515- and 516-MOFs), could efficiently assess food safety.346 Between the two, 516-MOF exhibited better biosensing ability toward vomitoxin and salbutamol. The 516-MOF-based electrochemical sensor not only demonstrated high detection sensitivity with low LODs of 0.70 and $0.40~\mathrm{pg~mL^{-1}}$ for vomitoxin and salbutamol in food but also showed excellent selectivity even in the presence of other structural similar interferences. Bougrini et al. fabricated an electrochemical sensor for the sensitive detection of tetracycline in honey based on a gold electrode surface modified with a MIP microporous-MOF.347 The sensor exhibited a wide linear range, ranging from 224 fM to 22.4 $\mathbf{nM},$ , and a lower LOD of $0.22~\mathrm{fM}$ . Similarly, a supersensitive MIP EC sensor was developed based on the structure of MOFs for the detection of phenylethanolamine A (PEA), a high-risk substance found in animal products.430 LODs for PEA as low as $1.0\times10^{-13}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ could be achieved using the developed MOF-MIP EC sensor. The recoveries of PEA ranged from $92.7\%$ to $108.4\%$ with varying PEA spiked animal urine samples, and the RSD was below $8.0\%$ . To realize multianalyte detection, Chen et al. presented an effective novel fabrication strategy for developing electrochemical sensors based on a nano MOF (nMOF) material (Figure 14).431 Using this strategy, nMOF was employed as the signal tag and encoded with different metal ions $\left(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\right.$ and $\bar{\mathrm{Cd}}^{2+}$ ) that exhibited distinct electrochemical reactivities. Furthermore, the nMOFs encoded with different metal ions were attached to modified Yshaped DNA probes. In the presence of CAP or/and oxytetracycline (OTC), the conformational change of the DNA resulted in the release of the signal tags. Subsequently, the circular strand-replacement DNA polymerization (CSRP) was triggered by the exposed sequences of the released nMOFDNA strands. This allows for the simultaneous realization of multitargeted analysis and signal amplification as the proposed electrochemical sensor achieved high sensitivity to CAP and OTC with LODs of 33 and $48\mathrm{\fM}_{\cdot}$ , respectively. Moreover, similar electrochemical aptasensor showed promising applications for the detection of kanamycin432 a 433 nd cocaine.
MOF-derived mesoporous carbon has also been applied in the preparation of electrochemical sensors due to their unique biocompatibility. Song et al. fabricated an electrochemical sensor for detection of OTC by using mesoporous carbon derived from an ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOF (525-MOF).434 The mesoporous carbon could bond with the OTC aptamer due to the strong bioaffinity. As a result, the fabricated electrochemical sensor exhibited excellent selectivity and sensitivity, with a LOD of up to $0.027~\mathrm{pg~mL^{-1}}$ . Most importantly, the aptasensor has been applied into detection of OTC in real milk samples. Similarly, Xiao et al. synthesized exfoliated porous carbons (EPCs) from isoreticular MOF-8 (IRMOF-8) derived porous carbon to improve analytical performances of electrochemical sensors.348 The proposed electrochemical sensor had LODs as low as $2.9\times\mathrm{1{\dot{0}}^{-9}\mathrm{~M}}$ for the detection of CAP in honey.
Pesticide residues in food are also a significant subset of hazardous substances in food products. However, the field detection of lower concentrations of pesticide residues in food requires highly sensitive detectors. Therefore, it is essential that we focus on developing sensing methods for detecting pesticide residues in the field. Recently, Deep et al. assembled nMOF $\left[{\mathrm{Cd}}(\operatorname{atc})(\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\right]_{n}$ (atc $=2$ -aminoterephthalic acid) on an indium tin oxide (ITO) electrode for use in pesticide sensing applications.352 The antiparathion antibody could be conjugated on the modified electrode surface of the nMOF via chemical bonding with pendent −COOH groups. The sensing of parathion levels was determined by monitoring the change in electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and exhibited excellent analytical performance for parathion in the range of $0.1{-}20~\mathrm{{ng\imL^{-1}}}$ . Additionally, the same research group reported similar research for the detection of atrazine with high sensitivity $\mathrm{(LOD~=~0.01~nM)}$ .353 The main difference between the two studies was the materials employed, with a silica $\left(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\right)$ -modified copper-MOF, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{BTC}\big)_{2}^{-},$ being used in the latter study.
Electrochemical sensors based on MOFs have been utilized in the rapid detection of heavy metal ions and have demonstrated satisfactory analytical performance. However, drawbacks of existing electrochemical sensors include poor stability and lack of quantifiable measurements. Utilizing the characteristic properties of MOFs allows us to explore an avenue for improving the analytical performance of electrochemical sensors. Wang et al. used UiO- ${\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PANI}}$ to fabricate a conductive electrochemical sensor by polymerizing the conductive polyaniline (PANI) polymer around particles of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ .367 The reliable detection of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions was performed and was based on the chelation mechanism between $\bar{\mathrm{Cd}}^{2+}$ and amine groups on the electrode. A wide linear detection range for $\mathbf{\bar{C}}\mathbf{d}^{2\bar{+}}$ concentrations from $0.5{-}600~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ was achieved, with minimum LOD of $0.3\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . Jin et al. synthesized a novel 3D microporous compound, $\left[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}\cdot\right.$ $(\mathrm{\dot{C}H_{3}})_{2}]_{4}[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{Hdpa})_{2}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{DMF}$ $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}-1\right)$ , where ${\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{dpa}}\ =\ 3,4{\mathrm{-di}}(3,$ 5-dicarboxyphenyl)phthalic acid, DMF $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ dimethylformamide, and applied the $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}-1$ in the fabrication of an electrochemical sensor.435 The sensor exhibited excellent sensitivity to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ as a result of preconcentrating ${\bf M}{\bf e}_{2}{\bf N}{\bf H}_{2}@{\bf M}\mathrm{OF}{\bf-1}$ for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ via ion-exchange. The lowest LOD achieved for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in river water was $1\times$ $10^{-12}$ M. Cui et al. prepared a DNA (GR-5) functionalized porphyrinic iron nano-MOF $\left({\mathrm{GR-}5}/{\left(\mathrm{Fe-}\mathrm{P}\right)_{n}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MOF}\right)$ as a probe to develop a sensitive and selective electrochemical sensor for detection of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ (Figure 15).436 The ${\mathrm{GR}}{-}5/\big(\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}\big)_{n}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ probe served dual roles and included recognition elements for $\mathrm{\hat{P}b}^{2+}$ and provided signal amplification of mimic peroxidase. As a result, the electrochemical sensor provided excellent selectivity and sensitivity for detection of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with a LOD as low as $0.034~\mathrm{nM}.$ Similarly, Wang et al. developed a 3D origami electrochemical device for sensitive detection of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in orange juice and apple juice based on DNA modified $\left(\mathrm{Fe-}\right.$ $\left.\mathrm{~P~}\right)_{n}$ -MOF.368
MOF-based electrochemical sensors have also been used to detect other contaminants, such as endocrine disrupting chemicals (EDCs) or illegal food additives, in food or water samples. In a recently published work, a composite material based on a MOF $\mathrm{\left(Cu-BTC\right)}$ , nanowhiskers of ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ silanized with (3-aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES), and ${\bf A u N P s}$ was prepared.360 Subsequently, this composite was used to modify a carbon paste electrode (CPE) to create a sensitive electrochemical sensor for BPA. The $\operatorname{AuNP}@\operatorname{MOF}$ composite electrode possesses a area of $0.167\pm0.010\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ , which is 2.3 times higher than the electroactive surface area of the original $\mathbf{\bar{(}}0.072\ \pm\ 0.004\ \mathbf{cm^{2}}\mathbf{)}$ result of the synergic effec matrix, which contribut al of BPA by about 2.5-fold $\mathsf{L O Q}$ developed electroch $37.80~\mathrm{\mM}$ and 126 mM, r ultra ensitive electroc BPA using hierarch modified to give a was 8.5 times that ${\mathrm{GCE}},$ apacity and electro ntly, cetyltrimethylam Ce-MOF was emplo ntration of BPA via hydrop alkane chain of CTAB. Based ation strategies, the design xhibited a wide linear range (0.005 to $50\mu\mathrm{M})$ $\left(2.0~\mathrm{nM}\right)$ for BPA in food samples al. presented an improved platform for the rapid detection gin aterials. In this sensing device ynthesized from copper nitrat $1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},$ and triethylenediamine (TED), $(1003~\mathrm{\dot{m}}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and provided abundan mobilization of tyrosinase o the sensitive detection of the performance of CuMOF-bas ple analysis, the sensor the detection of BPA and the at $13\ \mathrm{\nM}$ Zhang et al. employed rie mmobilization of cytochrome c $(\mathrm{Cyt}\mathrm{~c})$ to develop an electrochemical sensor for the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in drinks mpared to native Cyt c, ZIF-8 immobilized Cyt c increased the apparent enzymatic activity (about $128\%$ and sensitivity (1.4-fold) in the electrochemical detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Similarly, Yang et al. prepared a new Co-based MOF, $\mathrm{Co(pbda)(4,4-bpy){\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ (Co-MOF, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pbda}=3\cdot$ -(pyridine-3-yloxy)benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid), and utilized it as the active material for sensing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ . The electrocatalytic activity of the Co-MOF resulted in high sensitivity of the electrochemical sensor for the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and the LOD was $3.76\ \mathrm{\nM}.$ . Another advantage of using Co-MOF in such a system is that, due to its highly efficient intrinsic peroxidase-like activity, the Co-MOF is able to catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ producing hydroxyl radicals, which could oxidize the substrate, roducing a color change. In addition, many electrochemica on MOFs, such as MOF-14 and MIL-53(Fe), have been developed to detect ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2}^{-}$ in food samples.361,441
MOFs, as a class of porous coordination polymers, provide an avenue for the rational design of electrochemical devices, due to their synthetic versatility and ordered structure.442 Furthermore, high electrogenerated chemiluminescence, or electrochemiluminescence (ECL), emission efficiency, and amplified luminescence quenching effects can be achieved simultaneously, as a result of the electron transfer between the ruthenium-based MOFs $\left(\mathrm{Ru-MOFs}\right)$ and coreactants.443,444
Hence, the combination of MOFs and ECL detection can provide powerful analytical advantages and have exhibited great potential toward applications in food related analysis. Zhu et al. proposed a novel solid-state ECL “on−off” switch system based on the luminescence-functionalized Ru-MOF, $\{\mathrm{Cd}_{2}[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)}_{3}]{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n},$ $(\mathrm{dcbpy}\ =\ 2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylate), nanoflowers, and CdS quantum dots (QDs).377 In the rational sensing system, Ru-MOFs served as ECL labels and CdS QDs were the coreactant. The developed ECL sensor was successfully utilized for the detection of tryptophan, which is one of the main amino acids found in the human diet, with LODs for tryptophan as low as $0.33{\mathrm{~nM}}$ . Most importantly, the Ru-MOF could generate a stronger ECL intensity compared to $\mathrm{[Ru(dcbpy)_{3}]^{2+}}$ . This is a result of the unique characteristics of the Ru-MOF, including its well-ordered structure, good stability, and high catalytic efficiency, which result in efficient electron transfer. Similarly, the Ru-MOF could also be applied as an ECL sensor to detect melamine in dairy milk with high sensitivity.371
Rapid sensing of specific indicators of food spoilage can prevent the occurrence of food poisoning and health related problems. Chemiresistive sensing devices are attractive for developing portable detectors for indicators of food spoilage, which are primarily gaseous signaling molecules such as $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ $\operatorname{H}_{2}S,$ and NO. An emerging class of 2D MOF materials with excellent electrical conductivity demonstrated promise for application in chemiresistive sensing.445 Smith et al. developed a chemiresistive sensor based on conductive MOFs $\left(\mathsf{C u}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HHTP}\right)_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{(HHTP)}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ via self-assembly on shrinkable polymeric films of miniaturized graphitic electrodes (Figure 16).446 The chemiresistive sensor was used in the detection of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , and NO, which pose health hazards due to their toxicity and are molecular signals of food spoilage. The data analysis of chemiresistive sensors was performed using principal component analysis (PCA) to differentiate gaseous analytes, quantify the LODs, and discriminate between various analyte concentrations. The PCA results indicated that the developed chemiresistive sensor can distinguish between differing concentrations (80 ppm from 40 and $10\:\mathrm{ppm}$ ) of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and NO.
3.3.3. Surface Enhancement Raman Spectroscopy. Surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) is a technique that has been used in the rapid and sensitive analysis of chemical and biological hazards in food, agricultural products,447 and health care.448 In food safety quality control, a series of analytical methods based on SERS have been established for the detection of various risk factors, such as pesticides,449 illegal additives,450 veterinary drugs,451 mycotoxins,452 and environmental contaminants.433 However, construction of enhanced substrates that can improve the sensitivity of SERS detection is still a significant challenge.453
In recent years, substantial enhancement of nanomaterial SERS substrates has been developed to realize highly sensitive detection of contaminants in food.454 However, utilization of MOFs could further improve the sensitivity of SERS detection via molecule-specific preconcentration. Recently, there has been an uptick in the number of publications on the application of MOF encapsulated nanoparticles in SERS. In one study, Sugikawa et al. achieved a highly activated SERS composite by growing ZnO4(bpdc)3 around gold nanorods.455 Tang et al. successfully prepared a MOF-5 encapsulated gold nanoparticle $(\mathtt{A u N P}@\mathrm{MOF}.5)$ composite with a core−shell structure, in which a single Au nanoparticle core is coated with a uniform MOF-5 shell.456 The most significant result of this study is that the core−shell AuNP@MOF-5 exhibited a unique SERS response toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in gas mixtures. Li et al. introduced an AuNP-embedded MIL 101 for highly sensitive SERS detection.457 The SERS substrate was found to be highly sensitive for Rhodamine 6G and benzidine, and the LODs were 41.75 and $0.54~\mathrm{fmoL},$ respectively.
The SERS signal of methenamine could be significantly enhanced with the aid of $\operatorname{AuNP}@ $ MIL-101(Fe) composites. This phenomenon can be attributed to the syngeneic effect of increased “hot spots” and chemical enhancement via specific binding. Under optimum conditions, SERS analysis of methenamine exhibited excellent sensitivity with a $5.0~\times$ $10^{-10}$ M LOD and a wide linear detection range from $3.16\times$ $10^{-6}$ to $1.0\times10^{-8}\mathrm{M}$ . The $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MIL-}101$ -based SERS protocol has been successfully applied into the direct detection of trace methenamine in vermicelli.
3.3.4. Mass Spectrometry. Mass spectrometry (MS) is one of the most versatile analytical techniques for the analysis of small molecules, biological macromoelcules, and metal ions.458,459 It can also be applied to research on food safety, as the high sensitivity, high selectivity, and rapid response allow for the study of relative metabolism460 and toxicological evaluation.461 With the development of direct sampling for MS, as seen in matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization (MALDI),462 surface-assisted laser desorption/ionization (SALDI),463 and ambient ionization techniques including desorption electrospray ionization (DESI),464 direct analysis in real time (DART)465 MS has become a powerful tool in the determination and control of food quality and safety.466 However, a major factor affecting the analytical performance of MS is the interference from background matrices, which becomes more pronounced with the increased complexity of the sample. Thankfully, there exist are many strategies that can mitigate the interference problems. Of these, one of the most important and effective approaches in the preparation of matrices with low interference is powerful enrichment.
Recently, the scientific community has seen the rapid development of excellent nanomaterials for application as functional matrices to improve the analytical merits of MS and to give satisfactorily nontrivial results.467 MOFs are a class of materials that exhibit a promising perspective for application in MS analysis due to their excellent adsorption capacity, high stability, and low contribution to the background.468 The high adsorption capabilities of MOFs in the UV−visible range are of particular interest as they meet the requirements to serve as matrices for MALDI-time-of-flight (TOF) MS or SALDI-TOF MS detection. A good example of this was demonstrated by Chen et al., who synthesized a series of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs by using 2,5-pyridinedicarboxylic acid (PDC) and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DHT) as ligands.469 Among them, UiO-66- PDC and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{2}$ could be applied as functional matrices for MALDI-TOF-MS for the sensitive detection of small molecules, such as saccharides, amino acids, nucleosides, peptides, alkaline drugs, and natural products. Using the MOF materials in these MS studies results in low matrix interference, high ionization efficiency, and good reproducibility. Shih et al. used the cage-type MIL-100(Fe) MOFs as matrices in combination with SALDI-TOF MS to develop an analytical method for the detection of a neurotransmitter, acetylcholine.470 Gu et al. achieved a stable 2-D MOF nanosheet and utilized the 2-D MOF as a superior zero-background matrix in MALDI-TOF MS analysis to detect small biomolecules and pollutants in both positive and negative ion monitoring modes.471 Additionally, MOFs as a preconcentration adsorbent were applied for ambient ionization MS. Wang et al. used UiO66 in paper spray MS to detect antipsychotic drugs in blood and achieved high sensitivity with a range of $0.04\substack{-0.65\mathrm{~ng~}}$ mL−1.472
When utilizing direct sampling MS for the analysis of small molecules that affect food safety, the complicated food sample matrix has a significant effect on the sensitivity and accuracy. MOFs as functional matrices in MS analysis can result in molecular ion peaks without background interference.473 Moreover, due to their large surface area and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking structure, MOFs can enrich trace amounts of target analytes from food samples. Based on the characteristics mentioned, MOFs materials were designed as both functional matrices and preconcentration adsorbents for direct sampling in MS analysis for food safety, achieving satisfactory analytical performance. Lin et al. prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanocomposites via ZIF-8 coating on magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles.474 The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}.$ - 8 nanocomposites could be applied into MALDI-TOF MS as a functional matrix in the analysis of PAHs, with their characteristic host−guest property resulting in an interference-free background. Li et al. used $\mathbf{MIL-}10\mathbf{\bar{l}}(\mathbf{Cr})$ in tandem with fast detection DART-MS for the sensitive and quantitative analysis of triazine herbicides.475 With enrichment from MIL$101{\dot{(}}\mathrm{Cr})$ , the LOD for triazine herbicides was below $0.2~\mathrm{{ng}}$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and the LOQ was below $1.0~\mathrm{{ng}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}.$ , with a wide linear range covering more than 2 orders of magnitude. In addition, it is worthy to note that NPCs derived from MOFs also can be used as functional matrices in MS analysis. Shih et al. synthesized two NPCs (cMIL-53 and cCYCU-3) derived from MIL-53 and CYCU-3 and employed them as functional matrices for SALDI-MS analysis of polar compounds (such as carbohydrates, phenolic acids) and nonpolar compounds (such as phthalate esters (PAEs) and PAHs),476 which all are important factors affecting food quality and safety. cMIL-53 possessed large surface area $\left(114{\hat{5}}{\ m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ) and could act as
SPE adsorbent to enrich trace amounts of diethylhexyl phthalate. The excellent surface area of cMIL-53 enhanced the detection sensitivity of SALDI-MS analysis and LODs as low as $4.0\ \mathrm{ppb}$ could be achieved, meeting the requirement of United States Environmental Protection Agency for diethylhexyl phthalate in drinking water $\left(<6~\mathrm{ppb}\right)$ .
For the detection of functional components in food, the MOFs based materials for direct sampling MS have also exhibited excellent performances. Li et al. prepared a monolithic capillary of MOF $\left(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ and established a highly efficient phosphorylation analysis method that could be used in combination with MALDI-TOF MS (Figure 18).477
The developed method could be applied in the analysis of phosphoproteins, such as $s$ -Casein, an important functional component in milk. As a result of its excellent selectivity toward phosphopeptides, high tolerance for interferent molecules, and simple operation procedure, the monolithic MOF $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\bar{\cdot}$ ) capillary can selectively enrich phosphoproteins in complex milk samples. The results revealed that six distinct phosphopeptide peaks were observed in samples of nonfat milk, with no obvious interferents, after enrichment with the monolithic column of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . On the contrary, when the samples were analyzed without being processed through the monolithic column, only two low intensity peaks for phosphopeptides were observed.
## 4. MOFS FOR FOOD PRESERVATION
As food safety is an issue that has attracted significant global concern, there have been concerted efforts to develop highly efficient technologies for food preservation to ensure food safety.478 There are many factors to consider regarding food spoilage due to poor food preservation. Of these, microbial growth is one of the most pressing concerns. Therefore, it is imperative that we develop highly effective antimicrobial food preservation materials to minimize potential health hazards that can result from food spoilage.
Many research works related to the prevention of microbial growth during and after food preservation have been published.479,480 So far, several excellent examples of antibacterial MOFs have been explored and the results indicated that the bactericidal activities could be inhibited by the release of metal cations from MOFs.481 In one such study, Aguado et al. evaluated the antibacterial effects of two cobalt imidazolate MOFs (ZIF-67 and Co-SIM-1) for Gram-negative bacterial strains, Pseudomonas putida and Escherichia coli.482 The results revealed that the growth inhibition of both microorganisms was over $50\%$ at $\breve{5}-10~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ concentrations of both cobalt-based MOF materials. It is of notable interest that the antibacterial properties of both MOFs did have an extended effect which persisted for approximately three months. This phenomenon is attributed to the slow release of metal ions from the MOFs. While the cobalt-based MOFs demonstrate satisfactory performance in bacterial growth inhibition, silver-based MOFs seem to have a more significant antibacterial effect. Lu et al. achieved two silver-based MOFs, [Ag2(O-IPA)(H2O)]·(H3O) and [Ag5(PYDC)2(OH)],483 which were synthesized with $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ and two similar ligands: 5-hydroxyisophthalic (O-IPA) and 3,5-dicarboxylic pyridine (PYDC). These MOFs exhibited excellent antibacterial activities toward both Gram negative bacteria $\ (E.\ c o l i)$ and Gram-positive bacteria (S. aureus) with low minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) via regulating the sustained release of silver ions from MOFs. The MICs of $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{O}-$ IPA) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)]\cdot\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right)$ and $[\mathrm{Ag}_{5}(\mathrm{PYDC})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})]$ against $E_{\leftarrow}$ coli were in the concentration range of $_{5-10}$ and $10{-}15\ \mathrm{ppm},$ respectively, and were $_{10-15}$ and $15{-}20~\mathsf{p p m}$ for S. aureus. More importantly, $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{O-IPA})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}){\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{\bar{O}})\right]$ showed good biocompatibility in mice and did not exhibit significant cytotoxicity at concentrations of up to $100~\mathrm{ppm}$ . In addition, various novel MOF-based materials, such as MOFs loaded on natural or synthetic textiles484 and MOF templated films,485 have been developed and have exhibited remarkable antibacterial performance. Due to their excellent antibacterial properties, MOF-based materials have great potential application in food preservation.486
The use of naturally occurring food preservatives has multiple advantages, such as lower toxicity, fewer negative environmental effects, and better consumer acceptance.487 Many of these have been applied into food preservation.488 Nevertheless, it is known that most natural food preservatives are unstable in certain conditions and often cannot be directly used for practical applications. Therefore, it is necessary to develop encapsulating materials to improve the stability and allow for controlled release of natural food preservatives. Moussa et al. successfully encapsulated curcumin into cyclodextrin-MOFs (CD-MOFs)489 via noncovalent interactions. Most importantly, the stability of curcumin improved by a minimum of 3 orders of magnitude in CD-MOFs, when compared to free curcumin and curcumin $_{@\mathrm{CD}}$ in alkaline conditions $\left(\mathsf{p H}\ 11.5\right)$ . Recently, Wang et al. prepared a microporous MOF ( $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{3}(\mathrm{apy})]_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ bpdc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylate, apy $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 4{,}4^{\prime}$ -azobispyridine) encapsulated allyl isothiocyanate (AITC).490 AITC could be effectively captured and protected by the MOF. The results indicated that controlled release of AITC from the MOF could be triggered by increasing the relative humidity and the release could be sustained over several days. Subsequently, Lashkari et al. systematically investigated the feasibility of three MOFs (HKUST-1, $\mathbf{MOF-}74(\mathrm{Zn})$ , and RPM6-Zn) as novel delivery systems for the effective capture and controlled release of AITC molecules.491 The research results from the adsorption− desorption data indicated that the AITC molecules could be encapsulated and retained in pores of all three MOFs under low relative humidity $(30-35\%)$ and the controlled release could be triggered at high relative humidity $(95-100\%)$ (Figure 19). Therefore, the development of the AITC-MOFs delivery system for food preservatives has proven to be feasible based on laboratory scale results.
Controlled ripening of climacteric fruits is a critical step in improving fruit preservation as it provides consumers with high-quality products and reduces postharvest losses. It is known that ethylene gas has been applied as an effective regulator in the controlled ripening of climacteric fruits.492 However, direct application of ethylene gas for controlled fruit ripening may pose a safety risk. Therefore, Zhang et al. developed a MOF for the encapsulation and release of ethylene for controlled ripening in climacteric produce.493 A copper terephthalate MOF (CuTPA) with high porosity $\left(0.39\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\right.$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ was utilized to capture ethylene gas. The results indicated that $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of MOF could absorb and release up to $654\mu\mathrm{L}$ of ethylene, which created an atmosphere enriched with sufficient ethylene to regulate fruit ripening but also maintained safe levels of ethylene $\left(<27000~\mu\mathrm{{L}}{\mathrm{{L}}}^{-1}\right)$ . Most importantly, MOF− ethylene treatments accelerated the ripening process of fruits by reducing tissue firmness and facilitating the ripening-related color change. This result suggested that MOF encapsulated ethylene could be used for postharvest applications to encourage produce ripening just before the point of consumption.
Toxicity of MOFs is an inevitable issue when discussing food related applications. Recently, there have been increasing efforts to develop nontoxic MOFs for this purpose. One example of a nontoxic MOF is CD-MOF-1, $[(\mathrm{C_{48}H_{80}O_{40}})$ - $(\mathrm{KO}\bar{\mathrm{H}})_{2}]_{n},$ which was first reported by Smaldone et al.207 CDMOF-1 can be prepared from edible ingredients, including food-grade $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ with salt substitute (KCl) or potassium benzoate (food additive $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{E}_{212}\overline{{\mathbf{\Omega}}},}\end{array}$ ) in bottled water and Everclear grain spirit (EtOH). Recently, γ-CD-MOFs with high porosity and good biocompatibility were employed to enhance the stability of vitamin A palmitate (VAP).494 As a result of loading into the CD-MOFs, the VAP molecules curled into a preferred shape within the cavities, which resulted in a 1.6-fold increase in their half-life. In addition, Yang et al. presented two nontoxic MOFs, MOF-1201 and MOF-1203 (Figure 20).495 The MOFs were prepared from ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ions and L-lactate $\mathrm{[CH_{3}C H(O H)}$ - $\mathbf{COO^{-}}]$ . MOF-1201 and MOF-1203 had large surface areas of 430 and $160~\mathrm{~m~}^{2}~\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ and could encapsulate cis-1,3- dichloropropene, an important agricultural fumigant. A notable result of this study is that MOF-1201 exhibited a release rate 100 times lower than liquid cis-1,3-dichloropropene under the same conditions. The application of MOF-1201 as a degradable carrier demonstrates potential for the controlled spoilage of corn and fruit.
## 5. MOFS FOR FOOD PACKAGING
Food packaging plays an important role in the food industry and has improved food storage, transportation, and shelf life.496 Meanwhile, food packaging can also minimize the effect of physical, chemical, and biological factors affecting food safety.497 Therefore, there has been a great effort to develop novel food packaging technologies and materials.498 Emergence of new materials provides the chance to address challenges faced by existing food packaging, such as antimicrobial properties, removal of active molecules, and deceptively labeled food products.499
Though there have been few reports of MOFs materials in food packaging, MOFs, especially flexible MOF materials, possess special physical and chemical properties that have great potential to be developed into advanced and environmentally friendly materials for food packaging.500,501 Recently, Kosari et al. developed a series of chitosan-poly(ethylene oxide) (CSPEO) nanofiber mats with different ZIF-8 nanoparticle loadings $(3\%,\ 5\%,$ and $10\%$ ) via electrospinning.502 The results indicated that CS-PEO- $3\%$ ZIF-8 mat exhibited satisfactory hydrophobicity when subject to a water contact angle test and possessed appropriate mechanical properties. Most importantly, the CS-PEO- $3\%$ ZIF-8 mat demonstrated a $100\%$ antibacterial performance against Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli bacteria. As a result of the characteristic properties, CS-PEO- $3\%$ ZIF-8 mat was considered to be a promising functional material for food coating/packaging.
In addition, water and oxygen removal has always been a significant challenge when designing food packaging. Water and oxygen removal are necessary to improve the shelf life of various foods by minimizing oxidation reactions that result in food spoilage. Even after flushing food packaging with inert gas, the amount of residual water and oxygen is still significant, due to the food being able to trap air and water molecules. MOFs demonstrated the ability to scavenge residual water and oxygen and, thus, hold great potential for use in this application. Zhang et al. proposed a strategy to improve oxygen removal at room temperature using MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ encapsulated ferrocene.504 Nanocrystals of a powerful waterscavenging MOF-801 $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{fumarate})_{6}\right]$ have been shown to be able to harvest water even from $20\%$ relative humidity as demonstrated by a study conducted by Yaghi et al.46 Bae et al. successfully fabricated highly functional water barrier films based on MOF-801 and a hydrophobic cyclic olefin copolymer.505 Additionally, MOF-801 incorporation enhanced the films ability to resist water permeation and paved new avenues for the exploration of MOF-based membranes in various industrial applications, including food packaging.
Deceptively labeled food products are considered to be an important issue faced in food packaging, and identification of such products is necessary for the protection of food authenticity and ensuring food safety. Therefore, establishing intelligent standardized methods for identifying deceptively labeled food products is essential to meet the demand for transparency by producers and consumers in food production. In recent years, luminescent MOFs with various unique spectral characteristics have been gaining more attention as potential materials for invisible security labeling/encoding.506 Liu et al. encapsulated different dyes into green phosphorescent MOFs $\mathsf{\bar{[}C d(m{\cdot}B D C)(B I M)]}$ to achieve a series of MOFs with long-wavelength and long-lived luminescence.507 The dye-encapsulated MOFs exhibited luminescence emissions ranging from green to red. Furthermore, the optical properties of these dye-encapsulated MOFs could function as ink pads in the development of advanced anticounterfeiting stamps. Another interesting work was carried out by Wang et al.508 They achieved red, green, and blue (RGB) light-emitting nano MOF materials (nMOFs) by using $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaTb}^{\smash{\frac{3}{3}}+\mathstrut}$ , $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ and phthalic acid in combination with UIO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Furthermore, RGB light-emitting nMOFs were employed as inks to construct the colorful light-emitting boxes for information encrypting, while three black vertex boxes were set for positioning (Figure 21). The information regarding food anticounterfeiting could only be decoded with specific excitation with light at $275\ \mathrm{nm},$ but they were invisible to the naked eye. The studies above have provided a range of potential solutions to combat food anticounterfeiting and hold great promise in maintaining high levels of food safety.
## 6. MOFS FOR FOOD CLEANING
One thing that most people can agree upon is that a clean environment results in clean food. However, environmental pollution has become one of the major problems affecting food safety and thus affects human health.509 Many organic, inorganic and biological pollutants have been reported in water and soil, such as the herbicide, antibiotics, heavy metal toxic ions, POPs, and dyes. Once these hazardous substances are introduced into the environment, they can enter the food chain at multiple points and accumulate in organisms. While this may seem negligible, it does have an increasingly significant effect on animals that are higher in the food chain, i.e. humans.510 Therefore, the control of hazardous substances is a pressing need to prevent the development of persisting health issues as a result of toxic build ups from consumables. Due to the vast diversity of linkers, nodes, and topologies that are available, MOFs are uniquely positioned for this task as they can be tailored for different toxic elements. To date, many MOF-based materials have been successfully applied for the removal of hazardous substances in the environment. MIL-101,511 HKUST-1,512 ZIF-8,513 MAF-6,514 and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based $\mathbf{MOFs}^{515,516}$ are only a handful of examples of MOFs used for this application. MOFs have immense potential for adsorption and capture of hazardous substances in environmental media including water and soil via $\pi$ -complexation.517 Recently, many works have reported MOF-assisted removal of hazardous substances affecting food safety,518,519 such as pesticides,520 antibiotic drugs,5 environmental contaminants,522,523 dyes,524,525 metal ions,526 nanomaterials,527 and radioactive elements.528,529
Of course, the most exciting discovery is that MOFs can be directly applied to clean food-based oils and drinks, due to their excellent adsorption performance and lower toxicity.530 In one such study, Zhang et al. employed MOF MIL-96, $\mathrm{\small{[Al_{12}O(O H)_{18}(H_{2}O)_{3}(A l_{2}(\bar{O}H)_{4})(B T C)_{6}.24H_{2}O]}}_{2}$ , as a functional adsorption material for the defluorination of drinking water.531 The most notable fact was that MIL-96 functioned well in a wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range $\mathrm{(pH}=3\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{)}$ and at the maximum adsorption capacity for fluoride $31.69\mathrm{~\mg~{~g}^{-1}}$ . The defluorination efficiency and resultant aluminum levels with the use of MIL-96 were far superior to that of activated alumina or nanoalumina. Similarly, Ke et al. employed a MOF801 and calcium fumarate (CaFu) MOF composite as an adsorbent in the removal of fluoride from brick tea infusion.532 The results indicated that over $80\%$ existing fluoride in brick tea infusion could be removed within the first $\textsf{S m i n}$ with the aid of MOF-801. Valsova et al. evaluated the purification efficiency of Al-MOF, ${\mathrm{Zn-MOF}}.$ , and Ti-MOF for unrefined vegetable oils.533 The best performance for removal of free fatty acid and peroxide compounds was achieved with Ti-MOF (Figure 22). The maximum removal efficiencies of free fatty acid and peroxide compounds in vegetable oils with Ti-MOF were $64.8\%$ and $93.4\%$ , respectively. Additionally, Ti-MOF could be easily regenerated by washing with ethanol and thus can be recycled at least five times. Liu et al. exploited a UiO$66\mathrm{(NH_{2})}\ @\mathrm{Au-C}_{\mathrm{{J}}}$ s composite via cysteine-functionalized UiO$66\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ and employed the composite as an adsorbent for the removal of patulin (PAT), a mycotoxin that can be found in apple juice.534 The maximum removal efficiency of PAT from apple juice, $87\%$ , could be attributed to the presence of abundant active sites, including amine, hydroxyl, and carboxyl groups, on the surface of the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\bigl(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\bigr)@\mathrm{Au}{-}\mathrm{Cys}$ composite. The concentration of PAT in apple juice after treating with the composite could meet the standard recommended by WHO $(50\mu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ , while nutrients in apple juice, such as phenol and vitamin $\mathbf{C},$ exhibited no obvious loss.
Despite being able to improve the appearance of food, dyes can have a significant effect on food safety. One of the biggest concerns regarding dye use in food products is that the accumulation of dyes from food products could threaten human health. Yang et al. used UiO-67 as an adsorbent to remove illegal food dyes (Congo red and Malachite green) from aqueous solutions and food samples.535 The maximum adsorption capacities for Congo red and Malachite green were $1236\bar{.}\dot{9}\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ and $357.3\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Additionally, UiO-67 could be reused in at least seven cycles without significant degradation in capacity.
Additionally, MOF materials showed excellent performance in the purification of functional and active components of plant extracts for food application. For instance, Abdelhameed et al. used Cu-BTC MOF as a solid sorption phase to carry out the separation of chamazulene from chamomile extract,536 which has significant use in food related applications. Chamazulene, which was isolated by desorption from Cu-BTC, was analyzed by GC-MS for identification and determination of its purity. The chromatogram for chamazulene showed only one peak of $100\%$ which revealed that it was the isolated product in good purity. In addition, the catalytic ability of MOF materials was demonstrated to improve food function. Most recently, Xie et al. synthesized a catalyst by encapsulating the Keggin-type $\mathrm{C}s_{2.5}\mathrm{H}_{0.5}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ i n U i O - 6 6 . 5 3 7 T h e p r e p a r e d $\mathrm{Cs}_{2.5}\mathrm{H}_{0.5}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66$ catalyst was utilized for the acidolysis of medium-chain fatty acids in soybean oil to produce low-calorie structured lipids.
## 7. CONCLUSION AND PERSPECTIVES
In conclusion, MOFs have exhibited exciting potential in the field of food safety and have demonstrated their versatility through their ability to be used in various parts of the process. Though progress has been made, it is expected that MOFs will step into the new age and play an important role in food safety with the development of new MOFs.
First, a series of novel, sophisticated synthesis and modification methods for MOFs have been developed, but precise control over pore size and volume for specific applications is still a challenge. Therefore, more attention needs to be directed toward achieving high quality MOFs for applications in food safety.
Second, MOF-based materials have shown that they can provide great advantages in food analysis, including improvement of sample preparation, fabrication of sensing devices, and enhancement of detection signals. However, significant improvements need to be made regarding the selectivity of MOFs through chemical modification or by combining recognition elements such as aptamers, enzymes, and antibodies, which could provide a promising approach for the analysis of small molecules.
Third, emerging MOF materials have exhibited potential for use in food packaging. However, with the development of the food supply chain, MOFs or MOF based composite materials have attracted significant attention as a functional coating in smart food packaging for the controlled release of preservatives and monitoring of food safety. Therefore, multifunctional MOF materials that possess excellent adsorption, sensing, and stability properties should be further developed for the application of smart packaging.
Fourth, adsorption performances of MOF materials have been demonstrated and MOFs have proven to be effective in the removal of hazardous substances in the food supply chain as a result of their unique chemical and physical properties. The rapid reaction rates, stability, and lower toxicity of MOFs for the adsorption of a broad spectrum of hazardous substances in food are highly desirable. To meet these requirements, new multifunctional MOFs that can remove contaminants via varying modes must be developed.
Last, but not least, there needs to be a heavy focus on the toxicity evaluation of MOFs as there are few studies on their toxicology. Other than immediate toxicity effects, metabolic pathways need to be considered for the evaluation of MOFs that have the potential to be consumed as MOFs in food related applications and could be unintentionally introduced into the human body.
To conclude, while MOFs are a promising material that can be applied to various stages in the food chain to help improve food safety, there is much work that needs to be done, especially regarding the toxicity of MOFs.
