## Biographies
Pei-Long Wang obtained his Ph.D. degree in 2012 from Graduate School of Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences under the supervision of Z.-H. Ye. Since 2019, he has been a full professor at Institute of Quality Standards and Testing Technology for Agroproducts (IQSTAP), Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences (CAAS), His research interest focuses on the design of sensors based on novel nanomaterials, and miniaturized rapid screening method based on nanotechnology, mainly in the food safety field. He has authored 35 peer reviewed international journal papers. In addition, he has filed 4 Chinese patents.
Lin-Hua Xie obtained his Ph.D. degree in 2010 from Sun Yat-Sen University under the supervision of Prof. X.-M. Chen. After postdoctoral research at Seoul National University in Prof. M. P. Suh’s group from 2010 to 2012, and at King Abdullah University of Science and Technology in Prof. Z. Lai’s group from 2012 to 2015, he joined Beijing University of Technology as an associate professor. His research interests include the functionalization and separation application of new porous materials.
Elizabeth A. Joseph obtained her B.Sc. in Chemistry in 2015 from Nanyang Technological University, where she worked on the synthesis of novel inorganic complexes under Dr. So Cheuk Wai. In Fall 2015, she joined Dr. Hong-Cai Zhou’s group as a graduate student at Texas A&M University. Her research interest is focused on the development of materials optimization, materials for gas capture, as well as the development of MOF-based materials for use in biological applications.
Jian-Rong “Jeff” Li obtained his Ph.D. degree in 2005 from Nankai University under the supervision of Prof. X.-H. Bu. Until 2007, he was an assistant professor at the same university. From 2008 to 2009, he was a postdoctoral research associate, first at Miami University and later at Texas A&M University in Prof. H.-C. Zhou’s group; from 2010 to 2011, he was an assistant research scientist at the same university. Since 2011, he has been a full professor at Beijing University of Technology. His research interest focuses on porous materials for chemical engineering, energy, and environmental science. He has published more than 220 papers in peer reviewed journals, which have been SCI-cited more than 22000 times, and honorably, he was selected as a Web of Science Highly Cited Researcher in both 2017 and 2018. In addition, he has contributed 8 book chapters and filed 1 USP, 1 EP, and 40 Chinese patents.
Xiao-Ou Su obtained his Ph.D. degree at 2007 from Chinese Agricultural University under the supervision of J.-Z. Shen. Since 2001, he has been a full professor at Institute of Quality Standards and Testing Technology for Agro-products (IQSTAP), Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences (CAAS). He also is the leader of innovation team on “Evaluation on feed quality and safety” in CAAS. His research interest mainly focuses on feed and food safety. He has authored more than 60 peer reviewed international journal papers.
Hong-Cai Zhou obtained his Ph.D. degree in 2000 from Texas A&M University under F. A. Cotton. After postdoctoral work at Harvard University with R. H. Holm, he joined the faculty of Miami University, Oxford, in 2002. He moved back to Texas A&M University and became a full professor in 2008. He was promoted to a Davidson professor of Science in 2014 and a Robert A. Welch Chair in Chemistry in 2015. His research focuses on the discovery of synthetic methods to obtain robust framework materials with unique catalytic activities or desirable properties for clean-energy-related applications.
